00:14:23,481 --> 00:14:25,180
machine was needed.

248
00:14:25,181 --> 00:14:28,100
And so Pierre Michaud fitted a small industrial steam engine

249
00:14:28,101 --> 00:14:31,380
into one of his velocipedes.

250
00:14:31,381 --> 00:14:33,240
At full steam, the contraption was

251
00:14:33,241 --> 00:14:37,500
good for about 19 miles an hour.

252
00:14:37,501 --> 00:14:39,740
But while the bicycle is an obvious progenitor

253
00:14:39,741 --> 00:14:46,140
of the motorcycle, there are others, one of which isn't a

254
00:14:46,141 --> 00:14:49,580
machine at all. In very simple terms, a

255
00:14:49,581 --> 00:14:54,380
motorcycle is a mechanical horse. The kinship between biker

256
00:14:54,381 --> 00:14:58,300
and horseman is bred into the machine. It's most apparent in

257
00:14:58,301 --> 00:15:01,900
the way the particular riding positions have carried over,

258
00:15:01,901 --> 00:15:08,140
from horse to motorcycle. Americans have Western saddles.

259
00:15:08,141 --> 00:15:10,800
They stick their feet forward, and they don't use their feet

260
00:15:10,900 --> 00:15:12,380
very much for commands.

261
00:15:15,440 --> 00:15:16,580
In Europe, it's completely different.

262
00:15:16,680 --> 00:15:19,380
You ride your horses in what's called an English saddle.

263
00:15:19,480 --> 00:15:21,780
And so you're very much upright and with your heels

264
00:15:21,880 --> 00:15:23,780
underneath you, because you use the feet a lot more

265
00:15:23,880 --> 00:15:25,900
to command the horse.

266
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:27,900
On an American cruiser, the rider

267
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,500
is feet forward in a classic cowboy slouch.

268
00:15:33,640 --> 00:15:36,300
The European or Japanese sport bike rider

269
00:15:36,301 --> 00:15:38,300
is in a racing tuck like a jockey.

270
00:15:41,800 --> 00:15:45,300
Geography also had a hand in the design of motorcycles.

271
00:15:45,301 --> 00:15:47,300
For the twisting lanes of Europe,

272
00:15:47,301 --> 00:15:51,300
there was a premium on lightweight and sure handling.

273
00:15:51,301 --> 00:15:53,300
But the broad landscapes of America

274
00:15:53,301 --> 00:15:56,300
fathered the Harley-Davidson and the Indian Chief,

275
00:15:56,301 --> 00:15:59,300
highway cruisers with large displacement, low revving

276
00:15:59,301 --> 00:16:01,300
engines.

277
00:16:01,301 --> 00:16:04,300
I've tried to explain that a few times to English friends of

278
00:16:04,301 --> 00:16:07,500
friends of mine, they say, you know, why are Harleys the way

279
00:16:07,501 --> 00:16:08,500
they are?

280
00:16:08,501 --> 00:16:09,700
Why aren't they sportier?

281
00:16:09,701 --> 00:16:13,100
American bikes were really built to take people across big,

282
00:16:13,101 --> 00:16:16,540
open spaces without causing too much trouble.

283
00:16:16,541 --> 00:16:19,940
The English built very sporting, smaller, lighter bikes that

284
00:16:19,941 --> 00:16:23,660
were fun on the roads of England.

285
00:16:23,661 --> 00:16:26,260
By the turn of the 20th century in America,

286
00:16:26,261 --> 00:16:29,460
the machine with a bicycle lineage and cowboy attitude

287
00:16:29,461 --> 00:16:33,300
was the hottest thing on the road.

288
00:16:33,301 --> 00:16:35,440
Or what passed for the road?

289
00:16:35,540 --> 00:16:38,600
There were cart tracks, horse tracks, goat paths.

290
00:16:38,700 --> 00:16:42,740
And a motorcycle traversed those roads, quote, unquote,

291
00:16:42,840 --> 00:16:44,740
better than any four-wheel car.

292
00:16:44,840 --> 00:16:46,780
A motorcycle was also more powerful

293
00:16:46,880 --> 00:16:48,340
because it had less weight to carry.

294
00:16:48,440 --> 00:16:52,020
And there were big contracts for police, postal carriers,

295
00:16:52,120 --> 00:16:53,180
linemen.

296
00:16:53,280 --> 00:16:55,060
They all used motorcycles because they could get to where

297
00:16:55,160 --> 00:16:56,960
they needed to go.

298
00:16:57,060 --> 00:16:58,220
Cars got stuck.

299
00:16:58,320 --> 00:17:01,220
Cars were more unreliable, mainly because they had four

300
00:17:01,221 --> 00:17:06,320
four times as many parts as the average motorcycle.

301
00:17:06,420 --> 00:17:08,560
America's first production motorcycle

302
00:17:08,660 --> 00:17:10,760
came out in 1902.

303
00:17:10,860 --> 00:17:14,960
The so-called Camelback Indian, co-invented by Oscar Hedstrom,

304
00:17:15,060 --> 00:17:18,780
went places no other vehicle could.

305
00:17:18,880 --> 00:17:20,580
There was this famous hill.

306
00:17:20,680 --> 00:17:22,780
Up comes Oscar riding this thing,

307
00:17:22,880 --> 00:17:25,580
and he just zooms up this hill.

308
00:17:25,680 --> 00:17:28,420
And then he comes back down, and he goes up halfway.

309
00:17:28,520 --> 00:17:29,480
He stops.

310
00:17:29,481 --> 00:17:32,580
He starts and takes off again on the hill.

311
00:17:32,680 --> 00:17:35,720
And the press had never seen anything like this.

312
00:17:35,820 --> 00:17:38,820
An Indian was made in the day.

313
00:17:38,920 --> 00:17:41,520
While the Camelback proved a bike could climb,

314
00:17:41,620 --> 00:17:45,520
Glenn Curtis showed it could go flat out fast.

315
00:17:45,620 --> 00:17:49,540
In 1907, the then unknown tinkerer shoehorned a V8

316
00:17:49,640 --> 00:17:53,840
dirigible motor into a handmade frame

317
00:17:53,940 --> 00:17:58,540
and sent the rickety machine loose on the sand flats at Daytona.

318
00:17:58,541 --> 00:18:02,080
And they clocked him at 136 miles an hour.

319
00:18:02,081 --> 00:18:05,140
And you look at this thing, it has tires like your thumb,

320
00:18:05,141 --> 00:18:07,220
no suspension, no brakes.

321
00:18:07,221 --> 00:18:09,720
And here is the boy inventor climbing on this thing

322
00:18:09,721 --> 00:18:14,020
and doing 136 miles an hour.

323
00:18:14,021 --> 00:18:16,600
No vehicle of any kind would surpass his speed record

324
00:18:16,601 --> 00:18:19,420
until 1911.

325
00:18:19,421 --> 00:18:22,360
Curtis went on to build a production V-twin motorcycle.

326
00:18:22,361 --> 00:18:28,360
And later, of course, he made Aviation History with the

327
00:18:28,361 --> 00:18:29,360
Curtis Jenney.

328
00:18:33,360 --> 00:18:36,360
For the spectacle of speed, however, there was nothing like

329
00:18:36,361 --> 00:18:37,360
board track racing.

330
00:18:39,360 --> 00:18:42,360
This rare film gives a glimpse into the extreme sport of its

331
00:18:42,361 --> 00:18:43,360
day.

332
00:18:44,360 --> 00:18:48,360
Most race bikes at the time were pedal-started, but board

333
00:18:48,361 --> 00:18:51,360
trackers were geared so high for top speed that they had a

334
00:18:51,361 --> 00:18:55,300
that they had to be towed to start.

335
00:18:55,400 --> 00:18:56,800
They had no throttles.

336
00:18:56,900 --> 00:19:00,700
Once up to speed, the bikes ran wide open at upwards of 120

337
00:19:00,800 --> 00:19:06,100
miles an hour around a steeply banked wooden track.

338
00:19:06,200 --> 00:19:07,920
No brakes either.

339
00:19:08,020 --> 00:19:10,320
You slowed down by hitting a kill switch

340
00:19:10,420 --> 00:19:14,820
or by throwing your machine into a controlled skid.

341
00:19:14,920 --> 00:19:18,660
Just how dangerous all this was became clear on September 8,

342
00:19:18,760 --> 00:19:21,360
1912.

343
00:19:21,361 --> 00:19:23,020
Newark, New Jersey.

344
00:19:23,021 --> 00:19:26,160
This picture was taken just before it happened.

345
00:19:26,161 --> 00:19:31,160
An instant later, the lead rider, Eddie Hasha, lost control.

346
00:19:31,161 --> 00:19:33,900
A period diagram shows the path of the bike

347
00:19:33,901 --> 00:19:37,400
as it slammed into the guardrail, killing four spectators,

348
00:19:37,401 --> 00:19:40,400
then ricocheted back onto the track, killing a rider.

349
00:19:43,860 --> 00:19:46,600
Board track champion Hasha also died.

350
00:19:46,601 --> 00:19:50,440
The horrific accident made headlines,

351
00:19:50,441 --> 00:19:53,600
the first time that motorcycles were featured on the front page

352
00:19:53,601 --> 00:19:55,600
of the New York Times.

353
00:19:58,600 --> 00:20:03,600
The next year, the motorcycle was lampooned in Collier's magazine.

354
00:20:03,601 --> 00:20:07,600
Don't you know you can get the same sensations by tying fire

355
00:20:07,601 --> 00:20:10,600
crackers to your legs and sitting over an oil heater, the cartoon

356
00:20:10,601 --> 00:20:12,600
read?

357
00:20:12,601 --> 00:20:15,600
As for the workings of those controls and gauges which

358
00:20:15,601 --> 00:20:18,940
which have always baffled non-bikers, Collier said,

359
00:20:19,040 --> 00:20:22,240
you brake with your right hand, work the clutch with your left,

360
00:20:22,340 --> 00:20:25,640
and keep track of your oil and gasoline and electricity

361
00:20:25,740 --> 00:20:30,840
with the rest of your hands. But by then, the real argument

362
00:20:30,940 --> 00:20:34,560
against motorcycles had appeared in the form of the Tin Lizzy.

363
00:20:37,660 --> 00:20:41,860
Henry Ford's Model T had first appeared in 1909.

364
00:20:41,861 --> 00:20:46,360
Soon you could drive a car on a motorcycle budget.

365
00:20:46,460 --> 00:20:50,900
By 1915, some 2 million people had opted for four wheels.

366
00:20:53,700 --> 00:20:58,620
And finally, there was good news about those bad old roads.

367
00:20:58,720 --> 00:21:01,520
They'd learned how to build cement roadways.

368
00:21:01,620 --> 00:21:05,520
So both the infrastructure and the car got better and cheaper.

369
00:21:05,620 --> 00:21:08,520
And that really relegated motorcycles

370
00:21:08,521 --> 00:21:13,720
motorcycles to sort of recreation and even luxury status.

371
00:21:13,721 --> 00:21:16,520
It brought ruin to the motorcycle industry.

372
00:21:16,521 --> 00:21:19,800
In 1910, there had been about 150 companies making bikes

373
00:21:19,801 --> 00:21:21,700
in America.

374
00:21:21,701 --> 00:21:24,700
Almost all of them were put out of business by the Model T.

375
00:21:28,140 --> 00:21:30,580
Yet in a sense, the motorcycle had been liberated.

376
00:21:30,581 --> 00:21:37,580
They didn't need to be all that practical anymore.

377
00:21:37,680 --> 00:21:41,420
Once you might have chosen a bike for cheap transportation,

378
00:21:41,520 --> 00:21:44,520
but now you rode it just because.

379
00:21:44,620 --> 00:21:49,140
The thrill of speed, and the thrill of the speed of a

380
00:21:49,240 --> 00:21:51,840
motorcycle is to do with the fact that the wind is in your

381
00:21:51,940 --> 00:21:53,440
face.

382
00:21:53,540 --> 00:21:57,380
You are not in a motorcycle, you are on a motorcycle.

383
00:21:57,381 --> 00:21:58,880
You are not in a motorcycle.

384
00:21:58,980 --> 00:22:01,620
You are on a motorcycle.

385
00:22:01,720 --> 00:22:04,180
You are not going through the countryside.

386
00:22:04,280 --> 00:22:06,320
You are in the countryside.

387
00:22:06,420 --> 00:22:07,920
When it rains, you get wet.

388
00:22:08,020 --> 00:22:09,820
When it's sunny, you get warm.

389
00:22:09,920 --> 00:22:14,200
When there's somebody cutting grass, you smell that.

390
00:22:14,300 --> 00:22:17,060
In a motorcycle, you are moving across a nation

391
00:22:17,160 --> 00:22:19,400
and you feel like it.

392
00:22:19,500 --> 00:22:21,400
The cowboy connection again.

393
00:22:21,500 --> 00:22:25,880
Some people just crave the freedom of the saddle.

394
00:22:25,881 --> 00:22:29,120
But back in the 1920s, if you chose to ride,

395
00:22:29,220 --> 00:22:32,820
your choices continued to dwindle.

396
00:22:32,920 --> 00:22:34,980
During the 20s, Excelsior Henderson

397
00:22:35,080 --> 00:22:38,120
was one of the big three American motorcycle companies.

398
00:22:38,220 --> 00:22:41,420
The Super X and its oversized Springer front suspension

399
00:22:41,520 --> 00:22:46,940
was a favorite of racers and the police,

400
00:22:47,040 --> 00:22:50,980
until the Great Depression came along.

401
00:22:51,080 --> 00:22:52,840
The most direct effect, immediate effect,

402
00:22:52,841 --> 00:22:57,240
went out of business. That was owned by Mr. Schwinn of

403
00:22:57,340 --> 00:23:01,240
Schwinn Bicycle fame. And he looked at the tea leaves, the

404
00:23:01,340 --> 00:23:04,080
financial tea leaves, and he called a meeting and he said,

405
00:23:04,180 --> 00:23:06,680
gentlemen, today we stop production.

406
00:23:06,780 --> 00:23:09,280
With Excelsior Henderson out of the picture,

407
00:23:09,380 --> 00:23:11,900
there were only two companies left.

408
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:15,700
In the long run, there would be room for only one.

409
00:23:15,701 --> 00:23:22,200
one.

410
00:23:22,300 --> 00:23:25,540
Harley-Davidson and Indian.

411
00:23:25,640 --> 00:23:31,240
In the motorcycle world, they were Coke and Pepsi, Yankees

412
00:23:31,340 --> 00:23:36,160
and Dodgers, Hatfields and McCoys.

413
00:23:36,260 --> 00:23:41,560
The rivalry launched 1,000 tattoos and at least one poem.

414
00:23:41,660 --> 00:23:44,560
You'll never wear out the Indian scout or brother

415
00:23:44,561 --> 00:23:47,460
They're built like rocks to take hard knocks.

416
00:23:47,560 --> 00:23:50,460
It's the Harleys that caused the grief.

417
00:23:50,560 --> 00:23:54,400
And a reply from the Harley faithful says you.

418
00:23:54,500 --> 00:23:57,500
The fellow downstairs had a Harley Davidson.

419
00:23:57,600 --> 00:24:01,440
And one day I said to him, what kind of a motorcycle is that?

420
00:24:01,540 --> 00:24:03,440
He said, it's a Harley Davidson.

421
00:24:03,540 --> 00:24:05,440
And then he sort of grabbed me by the shirt,

422
00:24:05,540 --> 00:24:07,520
and he pulled me right up close, and he said,

423
00:24:07,620 --> 00:24:10,920
don't ever buy an Indian.

424
00:24:11,020 --> 00:24:13,220
But if two old motorcycle companies

425
00:24:13,221 --> 00:24:15,180
commanded such loyalty?

426
00:24:15,280 --> 00:24:18,020
Why did one fail dismally and the other become

427
00:24:18,120 --> 00:24:19,760
a runaway American success story?

428
00:24:22,660 --> 00:24:25,660
And why did one of the loveliest designs of the 20th century

429
00:24:25,760 --> 00:24:29,700
disappear from the American highway?

430
00:24:29,800 --> 00:24:31,300
Wasn't there enough room in America

431
00:24:31,400 --> 00:24:32,880
for both Harley and Indian?

432
00:24:35,680 --> 00:24:37,740
The two makes became the rivals,

433
00:24:37,840 --> 00:24:40,620
and they were rivals in racing.

434
00:24:40,720 --> 00:24:43,220
They were rivals in sales.

435
00:24:43,221 --> 00:24:51,220
Now, as it happened, the actual founding fathers were not enemies.

436
00:24:51,221 --> 00:24:58,220
In those less culturally sensitive times, the founders of Indian were called the Big

437
00:24:58,221 --> 00:25:00,179
Chief and the Medicine Man.

438
00:25:00,180 --> 00:25:04,460
Big Chief George Hendy was a former champion bicycle racer.

439
00:25:04,461 --> 00:25:10,380
Medicine Man Oscar Hedstrom was a whiz at engine design.

440
00:25:10,381 --> 00:25:16,380
Between 1902 and 1914, Hendy and Hedstrom built the world's

441
00:25:16,381 --> 00:25:20,380
biggest motorcycle company in Springfield, Massachusetts.

442
00:25:20,381 --> 00:25:24,380
Indian was the innovator.

443
00:25:24,381 --> 00:25:29,380
Indian set some speed records in America, and the Europeans,

444
00:25:29,381 --> 00:25:32,380
as they usually do, laughed.

445
00:25:32,381 --> 00:25:35,380
And so they sent a couple of Indians over to England,

446
00:25:35,381 --> 00:25:39,380
and they finished one, two in the Isle of Man races.

447
00:25:39,381 --> 00:25:43,180
A distant second in sales was the Harley-Davidson Motor

448
00:25:43,280 --> 00:25:46,120
Company of Milwaukee, Wisconsin.

449
00:25:46,220 --> 00:25:48,880
The company began producing a reliable single cylinder

450
00:25:48,980 --> 00:25:52,920
bike in 1903.

451
00:25:53,020 --> 00:25:56,840
But it wasn't until 1909 that they really hit their stride

452
00:25:56,940 --> 00:25:58,740
with a 45 degree V-twin.

453
00:26:01,340 --> 00:26:03,140
You can look at that bike today,

454
00:26:03,240 --> 00:26:08,680
and you can see in 2002 where that bike has come.

455
00:26:08,681 --> 00:26:13,420
There's a direct lineage between that V-twin and the V-twins of today.

456
00:26:16,300 --> 00:26:19,940
But then, the nature of the rivalry abruptly changed,

457
00:26:20,540 --> 00:26:22,840
due in part to another tragedy at the motor drum.

458
00:26:25,200 --> 00:26:30,900
In 1912, top Indian rider Jake DeRosier was gravely injured on the board track.

459
00:26:32,500 --> 00:26:36,500
After that, it's said, Oscar Hedstrom lost his taste for motorcycling.

460
00:26:36,501 --> 00:26:41,500
The day of DeRosier's funeral, Headstrom retired from Indian.

461
00:26:41,501 --> 00:26:45,500
Big Chief George Hendy soon followed.

462
00:26:45,501 --> 00:26:49,500
The rivalry was now between a corporation, Indian,

463
00:26:49,501 --> 00:26:52,500
and a family business, Harley-Davidson.

464
00:26:52,501 --> 00:26:57,500
During the 1920s, Harley and Indian matched each other bike

465
00:26:57,501 --> 00:26:59,500
for bike.

466
00:26:59,501 --> 00:27:04,500
But the companies were worlds apart.

467
00:27:04,501 --> 00:27:08,500
Harley-Davidson was driven by a hard-nosed business ethic.

468
00:27:08,501 --> 00:27:11,500
Indian was hobbled by mismanagement in the boardroom,

469
00:27:11,501 --> 00:27:14,500
pilferage on the factory floor.

470
00:27:14,501 --> 00:27:16,500
They were stealing the plant blind.

471
00:27:16,501 --> 00:27:19,500
There were guys just carrying stuff off from the plant,

472
00:27:19,501 --> 00:27:22,500
the Indian plant in Springfield.

473
00:27:22,501 --> 00:27:26,500
The company's in big trouble, so E. Paul comes in here

474
00:27:26,501 --> 00:27:30,500
and buys control of Indian. E. Paul DuPont bought

475
00:27:30,501 --> 00:27:33,740
bought Indian in 1929.

476
00:27:33,741 --> 00:27:36,000
Heir to the DuPont paint dynasty,

477
00:27:36,001 --> 00:27:40,200
he was also a motorcycle enthusiast.

478
00:27:40,201 --> 00:27:42,340
For the first time in over a decade,

479
00:27:42,341 --> 00:27:45,840
Indian was run by a gentleman biker.

480
00:27:45,841 --> 00:27:48,560
He brought a motorcycle ethic to the company,

481
00:27:48,561 --> 00:27:50,460
and he knew how to run companies.

482
00:27:50,461 --> 00:27:52,760
The files are full of handwritten notes

483
00:27:52,761 --> 00:27:54,300
that he wrote to the engineers.

484
00:27:54,301 --> 00:27:57,460
You know, rode the new scout, not happy with the power band,

485
00:27:57,461 --> 00:27:58,760
the brakes, the whatever.

486
00:27:58,761 --> 00:28:00,460
What can we do?

487
00:28:00,461 --> 00:28:04,760
Indian was back, though now in second place.

488
00:28:04,860 --> 00:28:07,700
And Harley was developing the machine that would keep them

489
00:28:07,800 --> 00:28:08,500
there.

490
00:28:10,300 --> 00:28:15,500
By 1936, it was ready, the EL model with the Art Deco lines

491
00:28:15,600 --> 00:28:19,720
and two-fisted rocker boxes on the engine, which was soon to

492
00:28:19,820 --> 00:28:20,920
be known as the knucklehead.

493
00:28:24,020 --> 00:28:26,920
The real innovation was between those knuckles.

494
00:28:27,020 --> 00:28:30,460
Older Harley and Indian engines were called flatheads,

495
00:28:30,461 --> 00:28:34,460
valves because the valves were beside the piston.

496
00:28:34,560 --> 00:28:37,700
Rotating the valves to a position over the piston heads

497
00:28:37,800 --> 00:28:41,500
allows for higher compression and better engine breathing.

498
00:28:41,600 --> 00:28:44,500
And breathing in an engine is the key to making horsepower.

499
00:28:44,600 --> 00:28:47,440
The more it breathes, the more gas and air you can put in,

500
00:28:47,540 --> 00:28:48,880
the more power it makes.

501
00:28:52,040 --> 00:28:54,460
For the first time in the long rivalry,

502
00:28:54,560 --> 00:28:57,960
Indian had no comparable bike.

503
00:28:57,961 --> 00:29:01,960
But there was life in the old company yet.

504
00:29:01,961 --> 00:29:06,960
In 1940, Indian unveiled a motorcycling classic.

505
00:29:06,961 --> 00:29:11,960
It came from the school of streamline design.

506
00:29:11,961 --> 00:29:15,960
Streamlining was the art of making objects look fast while

507
00:29:15,961 --> 00:29:20,960
they were standing still. For the 1940 model year,

508
00:29:20,961 --> 00:29:23,960
Indian designer Briggs Weaver achieved the effect on a

509
00:29:23,961 --> 00:29:24,960
motorcycle.

510
00:29:27,960 --> 00:29:35,400
Probably the single most distinctive shape in motorcycling, starting out with the front

511
00:29:35,401 --> 00:29:41,140
fender, the fully valenced fender, the Indian Chief running light, the chrome moto lamp

512
00:29:41,141 --> 00:29:41,841
headlight.

513
00:29:42,780 --> 00:29:46,160
It just sort of set the whole streamlined tone for the rest of the bike.

514
00:29:46,161 --> 00:29:52,540
And then you've got this sort of teardrop-shaped flowing rear fender that complements the front

515
00:29:52,541 --> 00:29:53,241
fender.

516
00:29:53,242 --> 00:29:59,880
It really is, to many people's eyes, probably the best looking of the Indian chiefs.

517
00:30:02,300 --> 00:30:07,100
Yet to many bikers of the day, that gorgeous sheet metal was just excess baggage.

518
00:30:08,100 --> 00:30:13,140
Any Indian restorer will tell you that thousands of them were thrown away and stacked up behind

519
00:30:13,141 --> 00:30:19,460
Indian dealerships, and mechanics who worked on them at the time were constantly taking them off

520
00:30:19,461 --> 00:30:20,280
and throwing them away.

521
00:30:20,281 --> 00:30:26,020
But the coming of World War II soon halted production of all civilian vehicles.

522
00:30:27,120 --> 00:30:29,780
The rivalry was now for military contracts.

523
00:30:31,440 --> 00:30:36,400
For the Army, Harley-Davidson developed a 750cc V-Twin.

524
00:30:38,220 --> 00:30:40,900
Indian came up with a 500cc bike.

525
00:30:41,300 --> 00:30:42,860
Not exactly a fair fight.

526
00:30:43,960 --> 00:30:46,580
The Army called for a 500cc bike.

527
00:30:47,780 --> 00:30:48,640
Harley built a 750.

528
00:30:48,641 --> 00:30:52,440
Indian played by the rules and built a 500cc bike that was

529
00:30:52,540 --> 00:30:55,040
pretty underpowered compared to the Harley.

530
00:30:55,140 --> 00:30:58,980
So Harley came out of the war in better shape than Indian.

531
00:31:01,080 --> 00:31:04,280
Harley had produced 88,000 military bikes,

532
00:31:04,380 --> 00:31:08,000
Indian fewer than 40,000.

533
00:31:08,100 --> 00:31:11,400
Yet after the war, Indian made one last daring play

534
00:31:11,500 --> 00:31:13,000
to steal Harley's thunder.

535
00:31:13,001 --> 00:31:15,600
thunder.

536
00:31:15,700 --> 00:31:19,800
E. Paul DuPont left Indian in 1945.

537
00:31:19,900 --> 00:31:24,740
He was succeeded by 36-year-old Ralph Rogers, a man

538
00:31:24,840 --> 00:31:27,540
with a vision to revolutionize American motorcycling.

539
00:31:30,660 --> 00:31:33,660
Right after the war, everybody wanted wheels.

540
00:31:33,760 --> 00:31:36,800
If you couldn't get a car, you wanted a bike.

541
00:31:36,900 --> 00:31:40,400
Problem was, as Rogers thought, you might be intimidated by one

542
00:31:40,401 --> 00:31:44,400
old-fashioned road burners.

543
00:31:44,500 --> 00:31:50,640
He was not swayed by the romance of the big road-going chief.

544
00:31:50,740 --> 00:31:53,540
He realized that these were old designs that had their roots

545
00:31:53,640 --> 00:31:57,080
in the 20s and 30s.

546
00:31:57,180 --> 00:32:01,520
In 1949, Indian introduced the motorcycle line intended

547
00:32:01,620 --> 00:32:04,120
to put every American on two wheels.

548
00:32:04,220 --> 00:32:07,760
There was a single-cylinder Indian arrow and a redesigned

549
00:32:07,761 --> 00:32:12,000
scout with a British-style vertical twin engine.

550
00:32:12,100 --> 00:32:15,500
The New Indians were hyped by the celebrities of the day.

551
00:32:15,600 --> 00:32:20,200
Cary Grant, Robert Ryan, and football star Bobby

552
00:32:20,300 --> 00:32:23,600
Waterfield and wife Jane Russell took photo ops

553
00:32:23,700 --> 00:32:26,420
aboard the New Indians.

554
00:32:26,520 --> 00:32:30,020
The marketing might have been sound, but the bikes were not.

555
00:32:30,120 --> 00:32:32,160
They were badly under-engineered and plagued

556
00:32:32,260 --> 00:32:34,820
by electrical failures.

557
00:32:34,821 --> 00:32:39,760
The new model line sat in mostly empty showrooms.

558
00:32:39,860 --> 00:32:43,320
And the dealers came in begging, threatening.

559
00:32:43,420 --> 00:32:44,760
I'm told they had to be locked.

560
00:32:44,860 --> 00:32:46,740
They had to lock the boardroom because these guys

561
00:32:46,840 --> 00:32:49,040
were out there beating on the door saying,

562
00:32:49,140 --> 00:32:52,840
bring us the motorcycles we can sell.

563
00:32:52,940 --> 00:32:56,800
Indians shut down the new model line in 1952.

564
00:32:56,900 --> 00:33:00,140
They continued making just one bike, the shapely but outdated

565
00:33:00,141 --> 00:33:05,640
chief until 1953.

566
00:33:05,740 --> 00:33:09,580
Then, the doors quietly closed on the old wigwam,

567
00:33:09,680 --> 00:33:12,480
as the Springfield factory was called.

568
00:33:12,580 --> 00:33:16,720
The building was demolished in 1984.

569
00:33:16,820 --> 00:33:20,360
Ralph Rogers has been vilified as the man who killed Indian.

570
00:33:20,460 --> 00:33:24,840
But his vision for the future of motorcycles was sound.

571
00:33:24,940 --> 00:33:27,500
This was the idea ahead of its time,

572
00:33:27,501 --> 00:33:31,840
Because what Rogers did in 1949 was exactly what Mr. Honda

573
00:33:31,940 --> 00:33:34,340
did in 1959.

574
00:33:34,440 --> 00:33:36,840
But of course, Honda's timing was better.

575
00:33:36,940 --> 00:33:42,140
And Honda's product was indescribably better.

576
00:33:42,240 --> 00:33:44,720
Indian's day was done.

577
00:33:44,820 --> 00:33:49,020
Harley Davidson was in sole command of the field.

578
00:33:49,120 --> 00:33:51,900
But the old rivalry between Milwaukee and Springfield

579
00:33:52,000 --> 00:33:54,900
has never quite died.

580
00:33:55,000 --> 00:33:57,000
Just ask a couple of partisan bikers

581
00:33:57,001 --> 00:33:57,900
as Peter Regan did.

582
00:34:00,700 --> 00:34:04,800
And I said, what was that Indian Harley thing all about?

583
00:34:04,900 --> 00:34:08,040
And the Indian guy said, he said, well, that's easy.

584
00:34:08,140 --> 00:34:10,140
He said, Indian riders could read,

585
00:34:10,240 --> 00:34:13,240
and Harley owners were the scum of the earth.

586
00:34:13,340 --> 00:34:17,180
And of course, the Harley guy thought

587
00:34:17,280 --> 00:34:18,460
it was the other way around.

588
00:34:22,900 --> 00:34:24,800
Back in the 60s, two things were

589
00:34:24,801 --> 00:34:28,040
to make your hands tremble on the steering wheel.

590
00:34:28,140 --> 00:34:32,880
One, a motorcycle cop on your rear view mirror.

591
00:34:32,980 --> 00:34:38,540
And two, an outlaw biker on his chopped hog.

592
00:34:38,640 --> 00:34:40,620
And chances are both these riders

593
00:34:40,720 --> 00:34:43,680
would have saddled up on the same brand.

594
00:34:43,780 --> 00:34:46,560
It's very interesting how Harley-Davidson strides

595
00:34:46,660 --> 00:34:49,500
both sides of an interesting cultural divide in America.

596
00:34:49,600 --> 00:34:52,560
On the one hand, you've got cops on Harleys.

597
00:34:52,561 --> 00:34:55,060
On the other side, you've got the bad boys on Harleys,

598
00:34:55,160 --> 00:34:57,800
the Hells Angels and so on.

599
00:34:57,900 --> 00:35:00,100
Stranger yet, they might literally

600
00:35:00,200 --> 00:35:02,700
have ridden the same bike.

601
00:35:02,800 --> 00:35:04,540
Since most of those bikes were probably bought

602
00:35:04,640 --> 00:35:09,540
at an auction, I wonder how many motor officers in the 60s

603
00:35:09,640 --> 00:35:13,380
have pulled over a guy that's riding a chopped out bike

604
00:35:13,480 --> 00:35:16,780
that used to be theirs, that he used to bust people on.

605
00:35:19,780 --> 00:35:22,520
So why did both policemen and bike

606
00:35:22,560 --> 00:35:25,460
ride Harley-Davidson?

607
00:35:25,560 --> 00:35:28,560
And how could the same machine play the dual role of cop

608
00:35:28,660 --> 00:35:29,360
bike and chopper?

609
00:35:33,400 --> 00:35:37,900
This rare film was shot for Easy Riders magazine in 1978.

610
00:35:38,000 --> 00:35:41,120
It shows two original members of the notorious Booze Fighters

611
00:35:41,220 --> 00:35:44,540
Motorcycle Club, riding their Harleys from LA

612
00:35:44,640 --> 00:35:45,880
to Sturgis, South Dakota.

613
00:35:49,360 --> 00:35:51,860
The Booze Fighters are credited with kickstarting the outlaw

614
00:35:51,861 --> 00:35:57,200
biker movement in 1947. But the outlaw heritage goes back

615
00:35:57,201 --> 00:36:00,640
even further. The term outlaw has several

616
00:36:00,641 --> 00:36:04,280
derivations. One is that it was just outlaw racers.

617
00:36:04,281 --> 00:36:07,580
They did not want to race within the confines of the American

618
00:36:07,581 --> 00:36:11,920
Motorcyclist Association. Founded in 1924, the

619
00:36:11,921 --> 00:36:14,720
American Motorcycle Association was the official race

620
00:36:14,721 --> 00:36:18,680
sanctioning body. But what if your pride and joy didn't meet

621
00:36:18,681 --> 00:36:20,480
criteria.

622
00:36:20,580 --> 00:36:23,420
Either their bikes were too big or they were the wrong types.

623
00:36:23,520 --> 00:36:25,380
And so they just formed their own race series,

624
00:36:25,480 --> 00:36:28,920
and they called it the Outlaw Race Series.

625
00:36:29,020 --> 00:36:31,720
This was one of the original Outlaw machines,

626
00:36:31,820 --> 00:36:35,940
a 1929 Harley-Davidson JD.

627
00:36:36,040 --> 00:36:40,040
In 1936, Harley itself had lobbied to have the two cam JD

628
00:36:40,140 --> 00:36:43,140
excluded from competition.

629
00:36:43,240 --> 00:36:45,940
It was so fast that it threatened Harley's new

630
00:36:46,040 --> 00:36:48,440
knuckleheads.

631
00:36:48,441 --> 00:36:52,340
Outlaw racers of the 30s were often called cutdowns.

632
00:36:52,440 --> 00:36:54,980
This was the first American custom,

633
00:36:55,080 --> 00:36:59,080
a bike stripped down to bare bones.

634
00:36:59,180 --> 00:37:01,180
The end of World War II brought a turning point

635
00:37:01,280 --> 00:37:03,580
to the custom bike scene.

636
00:37:03,680 --> 00:37:07,500
Former GIs were buying up ex-Army Harleys and Indians,

637
00:37:07,600 --> 00:37:10,400
which could be had for as little as 50 bucks.

638
00:37:10,500 --> 00:37:13,600
But no one wanted to leave them in military trim.

639
00:37:13,700 --> 00:37:16,440
Of course, they looked like Army surplus motorcycles.

640
00:37:16,441 --> 00:37:17,940
They were olive drab.

641
00:37:18,040 --> 00:37:20,440
They had these big fenders that kept the mud off.

642
00:37:20,540 --> 00:37:21,540
Well, they didn't want that.

643
00:37:21,640 --> 00:37:23,840
So they cut the fenders or bobbed the fenders,

644
00:37:23,940 --> 00:37:26,680
hence the name Bob Job or Bobber.

645
00:37:26,780 --> 00:37:29,480
On a Bob Job, the rear half of the back fender

646
00:37:29,580 --> 00:37:34,300
was bobbed or cut off to lose extra weight.

647
00:37:34,400 --> 00:37:36,300
The front fender was often just junked.

648
00:37:39,400 --> 00:37:43,200
The custom paint of the day was also a carryover from the war.

649
00:37:43,300 --> 00:37:46,440
Death's heads, eight balls, and pinup girls

650
00:37:46,441 --> 00:37:51,240
painted on war planes were tattooed on gas tanks.

651
00:37:51,340 --> 00:37:54,840
And who rode this new kind of custom?

652
00:37:54,940 --> 00:37:56,520
These were a bunch of ex-servicemen

653
00:37:56,620 --> 00:37:59,060
that had some spare time, had a little money in their pockets,

654
00:37:59,160 --> 00:38:03,560
had this sort of bellowing, fearsome, two-wheeled beast,

655
00:38:03,660 --> 00:38:07,600
and went out and raised a little hell.

656
00:38:07,700 --> 00:38:10,960
One that was in the Bruce Fighters was a veteran.

657
00:38:11,060 --> 00:38:12,960
And we all come back with the same thing in mind.

658
00:38:13,060 --> 00:38:15,900
Jesus, now we can kind of play and play

659
00:38:15,901 --> 00:38:19,200
do hairy things and nobody's shooting at our .

660
00:38:19,300 --> 00:38:23,000
That made a hell of a difference in life, you know?

661
00:38:23,100 --> 00:38:24,580
When there's no bullets flying, by God,

662
00:38:24,680 --> 00:38:27,440
you can have a pretty good time.

663
00:38:27,540 --> 00:38:29,340
Out for a good time.

664
00:38:29,440 --> 00:38:32,020
That's all the booze fighters claimed they were up to on July

665
00:38:32,120 --> 00:38:34,880
4, 1947.

666
00:38:34,980 --> 00:38:37,420
The motorcycle club was in Hollister, California,

667
00:38:37,520 --> 00:38:40,020
for the annual AMA races.

668
00:38:40,120 --> 00:38:44,420
Barred from the official events, the bikers staged their own.

669
00:38:44,421 --> 00:38:47,760
The local fuzz blocked off the main drag there

670
00:38:47,860 --> 00:38:51,060
for a full block so we could have drag races,

671
00:38:51,160 --> 00:38:54,160
burn circles, figure eights, and all this kind of wild west

672
00:38:55,260 --> 00:38:57,560
And then the goddamn state highway come along.

673
00:38:57,660 --> 00:39:00,460
They don't know that the city cops blocked it off

674
00:39:00,560 --> 00:39:02,180
with the barricades on each end.

675
00:39:02,280 --> 00:39:04,580
They think we done it.

676
00:39:04,680 --> 00:39:06,540
A rumor spread that the booze fighters

677
00:39:06,640 --> 00:39:08,480
had taken over the town.

678
00:39:08,580 --> 00:39:10,520
And what seemed like photographic evidence

679
00:39:10,620 --> 00:39:13,120
wasn't long in coming.

680
00:39:13,121 --> 00:39:15,520
Life magazine come down, and they got these fellas

681
00:39:15,620 --> 00:39:18,560
to pose for these wild pictures.

682
00:39:18,660 --> 00:39:21,060
And they connected with the booze fighters.

683
00:39:21,160 --> 00:39:24,660
And when Life magazine published it, it took off then.

684
00:39:24,760 --> 00:39:27,560
Nobody, you couldn't stop it.

685
00:39:27,660 --> 00:39:32,180
The man posed on the Bob job wasn't even a biker.

686
00:39:32,280 --> 00:39:34,200
Yet motorcycling has never really recovered

687
00:39:34,300 --> 00:39:37,180
from the bad publicity.

688
00:39:37,280 --> 00:39:40,920
It didn't help that in 1954, Hollywood gave its take

689
00:39:40,921 --> 00:39:43,420
take on what happened at Hollister.

690
00:39:43,520 --> 00:39:45,020
They made the picture of the Wild One.

691
00:39:45,120 --> 00:39:45,920
The Wild One.

692
00:39:46,020 --> 00:39:47,920
It was taken after the booze fighters.

693
00:39:48,020 --> 00:39:51,760
And of course, you know, Marlon Brando played the lead,

694
00:39:51,860 --> 00:39:55,160
and Lee Marvin was Wino Willie.

695
00:39:55,260 --> 00:39:58,200
And Wino was going to be a technical director,

696
00:39:58,300 --> 00:40:00,340
but he got drunk the night before.

697
00:40:00,440 --> 00:40:03,380
And so, uh, so he wasn't a technical director.

698
00:40:03,480 --> 00:40:04,280
Advisor.

699
00:40:07,580 --> 00:40:10,380
The Wild One spawned countless other biker flicks.

700
00:40:10,381 --> 00:40:13,020
In the eyes of moviegoers, at least,

701
00:40:13,120 --> 00:40:15,020
many riders were real outlaws.

702
00:40:18,420 --> 00:40:20,720
On the opposite side of the law, motorcycles

703
00:40:20,820 --> 00:40:24,120
were also a symbol, in this case of unbending authority.

704
00:40:27,140 --> 00:40:30,840
Harley-Davidson was the police motorcycle by default,

705
00:40:30,940 --> 00:40:35,180
ever since the demise of Indian in 1953.

706
00:40:35,280 --> 00:40:39,080
Not that it wasn't supremely adaptable for the job.

707
00:40:39,081 --> 00:40:43,080
It was very much suited at that time for police work

708
00:40:43,180 --> 00:40:46,380
in terms that it afforded the most comfort,

709
00:40:46,480 --> 00:40:50,020
it afforded the most power, and it was the most adaptive

710
00:40:50,120 --> 00:40:53,020
to the needs of law enforcement.

711
00:40:53,120 --> 00:40:56,300
While outlaw racers stripped their machines down,

712
00:40:56,400 --> 00:40:59,460
the police piled it on.

713
00:40:59,560 --> 00:41:03,300
A police bike had to carry a bulky two-way radio mounted

714
00:41:03,400 --> 00:41:05,240
above the rear fender.

715
00:41:05,340 --> 00:41:09,080
Microphone and speaker were mounted on the left handlebar.

716
00:41:09,081 --> 00:41:13,080
The siren of those days was known as a growler.

717
00:41:13,081 --> 00:41:17,080
This is one of the old-style mechanical sirens that was used

718
00:41:17,081 --> 00:41:20,080
in the 60s eras on the Harley-Davidson motorcycle.

719
00:41:20,081 --> 00:41:24,080
The principle was that this portion here rested on the rear tire

720
00:41:24,081 --> 00:41:28,080
of the motorcycle, and as the motorcycle went fast,

721
00:41:28,081 --> 00:41:32,080
it made a high-pitched siren sound, and as it went slower,

722
00:41:32,081 --> 00:41:34,080
it made a growling sound.

723
00:41:34,081 --> 00:41:38,080
Hence the nickname of this was called the growler.

724
00:41:39,080 --> 00:41:42,680
The famous look of the motor officer was highly traditional,

725
00:41:42,681 --> 00:41:46,220
derived in large part from the mounted police.

726
00:41:46,221 --> 00:41:49,720
The tall spit-shine boots are those of cavalry officers,

727
00:41:49,721 --> 00:41:52,020
as are the tucked-in riding breeches,

728
00:41:52,021 --> 00:41:54,720
tailored to the needs of the motorcycle cop.

729
00:41:54,721 --> 00:41:57,580
There's a lot of safety features built into this uniform,

730
00:41:57,581 --> 00:42:00,520
like the double thickness on the knees on the pants

731
00:42:00,521 --> 00:42:05,140
and the double thickness on the rear portion of the pants.

732
00:42:05,141 --> 00:42:08,280
All of that's for safety reasons.

733
00:42:08,281 --> 00:42:10,740
Among policemen, there's a great deal of prestige

734
00:42:10,741 --> 00:42:12,620
in being a motor officer.

735
00:42:12,621 --> 00:42:15,720
They are the most visible officers on the road.

736
00:42:15,721 --> 00:42:17,720
In traffic, they are often the first on the scene

737
00:42:17,721 --> 00:42:18,920
of an accident or crime.

738
00:42:23,260 --> 00:42:26,060
Going by riding prowess and time in the saddle,

739
00:42:26,061 --> 00:42:29,700
the cop is the ultimate biker.

740
00:42:29,701 --> 00:42:33,800
And yes, his primary duty is to hand out tickets.

741
00:42:33,801 --> 00:42:35,880
A day in the life of a motor officer

742
00:42:35,881 --> 00:42:39,820
would consist of writing a good number of tickets.

743
00:42:39,821 --> 00:42:43,760
So it requires a lot of starting and stopping, accelerating,

744
00:42:43,761 --> 00:42:45,300
decelerating.

745
00:42:45,301 --> 00:42:46,800
It's a busy day, in my opinion.

746
00:42:49,600 --> 00:42:52,600
On the flip side of motorcycling in the 60s,

747
00:42:52,601 --> 00:42:55,340
there was a new scene.

748
00:42:55,341 --> 00:42:59,440
Now there were self-proclaimed outlaws on wheels, the Hell's

749
00:42:59,441 --> 00:43:00,940
Angels and their many imitators.

750
00:43:00,941 --> 00:43:05,940
And what they rode was a radical new kind of custom.

751
00:43:07,940 --> 00:43:13,940
Mile-long forks, sky-high handlebars, ear-splitting pipes,

752
00:43:13,941 --> 00:43:17,940
eye-popping paint, the chopper.

753
00:43:21,940 --> 00:43:24,940
Each one of these machines was a personal expression of its

754
00:43:24,941 --> 00:43:26,940
owner.

755
00:43:26,941 --> 00:43:30,940
But though no two were exactly alike, there were many

756
00:43:30,941 --> 00:43:33,840
were some rules of engagement.

757
00:43:33,841 --> 00:43:36,240
Choppers were usually Harley-Davidson's,

758
00:43:36,241 --> 00:43:40,340
and often had rigid rear ends.

759
00:43:40,341 --> 00:43:44,580
Rigids had no shock absorbers at the rear axle.

760
00:43:44,581 --> 00:43:46,640
It made for a kidney-jolting ride,

761
00:43:46,641 --> 00:43:49,100
but what bikers got in return was a feeling of being

762
00:43:49,101 --> 00:43:52,540
hardwired to their bikes.

763
00:43:52,541 --> 00:43:54,540
I like that positive feel of a rigid,

764
00:43:54,541 --> 00:43:56,300
where you've got to pay attention,

765
00:43:56,301 --> 00:44:00,440
and you're kind of one with the bike, literally.

766
00:44:00,441 --> 00:44:03,200
As for those exaggerated front forks,

767
00:44:03,201 --> 00:44:06,580
so long they made the wheels look tiny by comparison,

768
00:44:06,581 --> 00:44:09,940
they were yet another legacy of World War II.

769
00:44:09,941 --> 00:44:11,680
I think this front end right here,

770
00:44:11,681 --> 00:44:15,440
which was a Harley-Davidson military XA front end,

771
00:44:15,441 --> 00:44:17,900
which is a couple inches longer,

772
00:44:17,901 --> 00:44:21,200
so it made the bike sit up higher for better ground clearance.

773
00:44:21,201 --> 00:44:23,200
And I think that's probably what a lot of guys used

774
00:44:23,201 --> 00:44:26,100
to raise their bike up, and it gave it

775
00:44:26,101 --> 00:44:28,200
a little bit more radical look.

776
00:44:28,201 --> 00:44:30,440
To go even farther, guys used to take

777
00:44:30,441 --> 00:44:34,280
early forward radius rods, which had the same general shape,

778
00:44:34,380 --> 00:44:36,880
and they could hack these off and plug them right in there,

779
00:44:36,980 --> 00:44:38,680
and they'd make the front end like this long.

780
00:44:41,720 --> 00:44:44,580
And once those forks started stretching out,

781
00:44:44,680 --> 00:44:47,420
all bets were off.

782
00:44:47,520 --> 00:44:50,560
It's the typical American way, man.

783
00:44:50,660 --> 00:44:55,300
If three inches good, then 20 inches is like way better.

784
00:44:55,400 --> 00:44:57,300
Jimmy's got a three-over front end.

785
00:44:57,400 --> 00:44:59,000
Well, screw him.

786
00:44:59,001 --> 00:45:03,440
I want a six-over, you know, and I want big, giant bars.

787
00:45:03,540 --> 00:45:04,740
You know, there's no way Jimmy's going

788
00:45:04,840 --> 00:45:07,940
to have a better bike than me.

789
00:45:08,040 --> 00:45:11,540
Bikers have one-upped each other ever since.

790
00:45:11,640 --> 00:45:13,440
This film, shot in the late 70s,

791
00:45:13,540 --> 00:45:16,360
shows an extreme moment in the evolution of choppers.

792
00:45:20,260 --> 00:45:24,660
By then, there were radically stepped seats,

793
00:45:24,760 --> 00:45:28,600
tall sissy bars for the passenger to kick back on,

794
00:45:28,601 --> 00:45:32,500
Those high handlebars called ape hangers,

795
00:45:32,600 --> 00:45:36,400
and at the other extreme, short flat handlebars called drag

796
00:45:36,500 --> 00:45:42,540
bars, stretched gas tanks, wild paint jobs,

797
00:45:42,640 --> 00:45:46,460
and acres of gleaming chrome completed the outlaw ride.

798
00:45:49,060 --> 00:45:53,960
The chopper is one of the most enduring of custom styles.

799
00:45:54,060 --> 00:45:57,360
Cues from the 60s and 70s have found their way to modern Harley

800
00:45:57,361 --> 00:46:00,400
to modern Harley-Davidson's.

801
00:46:00,500 --> 00:46:03,460
And today, one of the most coveted rides of all

802
00:46:03,560 --> 00:46:08,300
is a West Coast chopper by old-school builder Jesse James.

803
00:46:08,400 --> 00:46:10,300
I like them to look like there's not enough parts there

804
00:46:10,400 --> 00:46:12,100
to make it run.

805
00:46:12,200 --> 00:46:14,120
A couple major components are missing.

806
00:46:14,220 --> 00:46:15,020
They must be.

807
00:46:15,120 --> 00:46:17,620
That bike can't even run.

808
00:46:17,720 --> 00:46:19,920
And people are standing around having an argument whether it

809
00:46:20,020 --> 00:46:23,420
runs or not, and then I jump on it, do a big burnout,

810
00:46:23,520 --> 00:46:24,720
and they're gone.

811
00:46:24,721 --> 00:46:28,720
Back in the 60s and 70s, while cops and bikers

812
00:46:28,820 --> 00:46:30,660
were loyal to their Harley Davidsons,

813
00:46:30,760 --> 00:46:34,160
the motorcycle world was completely changing around them.

814
00:46:38,660 --> 00:46:41,300
You may not have known it if you just rode Harleys,

815
00:46:41,400 --> 00:46:44,680
but the 50s and 60s were a time of dizzying change.

816
00:46:46,900 --> 00:46:50,020
The British came to visit.

817
00:46:50,120 --> 00:46:53,040
The Japanese came to stay.

818
00:46:53,041 --> 00:46:59,440
Scooters came and went. And it all began in 1948 with a man

819
00:46:59,441 --> 00:47:06,080
in a bathing suit and swim cap. American rider Roley Free is seen

820
00:47:06,081 --> 00:47:10,560
here practicing for a record attempt on a British maid Vincent.

821
00:47:10,561 --> 00:47:14,800
The stomach down riding position was optimal for minimizing wind

822
00:47:14,801 --> 00:47:17,360
resistance, even though the rider couldn't see where he was

823
00:47:17,361 --> 00:47:22,640
going. But on September 13, 1948 at the

824
00:47:22,641 --> 00:47:27,440
at the Bonneville Salt Flats, Free went even further.

825
00:47:27,540 --> 00:47:29,940
He went like 149.9.

826
00:47:30,040 --> 00:47:32,480
He just couldn't get faster than that.

827
00:47:32,580 --> 00:47:34,980
So he took off his leathers and his boots,

828
00:47:35,080 --> 00:47:37,680
stripped down to essentially his underwear, a bathing suit,

829
00:47:37,780 --> 00:47:39,800
and laid on the bike and broke 150.

830
00:47:39,900 --> 00:47:43,600
That gave him the aerodynamics he needed just to get over there.

831
00:47:43,700 --> 00:47:47,740
In a chase car, a photographer captured the moment.

832
00:47:47,840 --> 00:47:49,600
Life magazine printed that picture,

833
00:47:49,700 --> 00:47:51,400
and everybody in America instantly

834
00:47:51,401 --> 00:47:53,800
that there was this motorcycle from England called a Vincent

835
00:47:53,801 --> 00:47:57,000
that would go 150 miles an hour.

836
00:47:57,001 --> 00:48:00,200
And that bike cemented the Vincent legend.

837
00:48:00,201 --> 00:48:03,040
It was the fastest bike in the world,

838
00:48:03,041 --> 00:48:07,040
and certainly it looked the part.

839
00:48:07,041 --> 00:48:10,760
At a time when a stock Harley could do maybe 90 miles an hour,

840
00:48:10,761 --> 00:48:15,800
the Honest 150 on a Vincent speedometer was mind-blowing.

841
00:48:15,801 --> 00:48:18,800
This was a rocket ship to the moon, virtually.

842
00:48:18,801 --> 00:48:21,240
150 mile an hour, that was a magic number.

843
00:48:22,840 --> 00:48:25,560
Vincent's, like this 1953 Black Shadow,

844
00:48:25,900 --> 00:48:27,760
were elite, expensive machines.

845
00:48:28,540 --> 00:48:30,520
Not so the other British motorcycles.

846
00:48:31,940 --> 00:48:34,560
The pound had been devalued after World War II,

847
00:48:34,960 --> 00:48:36,780
making British wheels relatively cheap.

848
00:48:38,220 --> 00:48:39,900
The cry was export or die.

849
00:48:40,240 --> 00:48:42,300
So that's where you got a lot of the sports cars,

850
00:48:42,480 --> 00:48:45,680
the Austin Heelys, the Bug Eye Sprites, the Triumphs,

851
00:48:45,681 --> 00:48:47,800
and that's where you got a lot of the motorcycles,

852
00:48:47,801 --> 00:48:51,300
Triumph, Norton, BSA, Ariel, AJS.

853
00:48:51,400 --> 00:48:52,540
I mean, the list goes on and on.

854
00:48:55,300 --> 00:48:57,080
Many of these bikes could do the ton,

855
00:48:57,180 --> 00:48:59,540
as the British called the 100 mile an hour mark,

856
00:48:59,640 --> 00:49:01,180
right out of the crate.

857
00:49:01,280 --> 00:49:04,880
And they were covered with metallic eye candy.

858
00:49:04,980 --> 00:49:09,720
The British always had very good looking components

859
00:49:09,820 --> 00:49:11,020
on their bike.

860
00:49:11,120 --> 00:49:11,960
They had nice chrome.

861
00:49:12,060 --> 00:49:14,320
They had beautifully polished cases.

862
00:49:14,420 --> 00:49:17,800
They had a very nice aesthetic sense.

863
00:49:17,801 --> 00:49:22,800
Throughout the 50s, the British had bragging rights on Main Street.

864
00:49:22,801 --> 00:49:28,800
After all, wasn't Marlon Brando and the Wild One riding a British-made Triumph?

865
00:49:28,801 --> 00:49:34,800
And here, a motorcycle races a hot rod. You asked for it.

866
00:49:34,801 --> 00:49:40,800
For a television showdown between a hot rod and a motorcycle, a Triumph Thunderbird was chosen.

867
00:49:40,801 --> 00:49:42,800
Go!

868
00:49:42,801 --> 00:49:43,700
Go!

869
00:49:57,340 --> 00:49:58,440
The Triumph won.

870
00:50:01,660 --> 00:50:04,820
The upstart British even got a rise out of a certain American

871
00:50:04,920 --> 00:50:06,620
motorcycle company.

872
00:50:06,720 --> 00:50:09,920
In 1957, the first Harley-Davidson Sportster

873
00:50:10,020 --> 00:50:12,060
appeared.

874
00:50:12,061 --> 00:50:15,760
The Sportster had a race bred engine and a lightweight frame

875
00:50:15,860 --> 00:50:18,960
and a most un-Harley-like foot shift.

876
00:50:19,060 --> 00:50:21,740
It's no coincidence that the original Sportsters

877
00:50:21,840 --> 00:50:26,840
had the foot shift on the right the way the British bikes did,

878
00:50:26,940 --> 00:50:31,440
whilst the big twin Harley had the shift on the left.

879
00:50:31,540 --> 00:50:34,780
The Sportster was designed to meet the incoming competition.

880
00:50:37,820 --> 00:50:40,620
Then along came the 60s, changing

881
00:50:40,621 --> 00:50:43,980
America's mind about a lot of things, including how to have

882
00:50:43,981 --> 00:50:45,020
fun on two wheels.

883
00:50:45,021 --> 00:50:52,760
Hey, hey, Vespa!

884
00:50:52,761 --> 00:50:56,460
Vespa scooters, the carefree little machines arrived on

885
00:50:56,461 --> 00:51:00,480
American shores in the early 60s.

886
00:51:00,481 --> 00:51:05,680
But this curvy Italian beauty had a not so innocent past.

887
00:51:05,681 --> 00:51:08,520
Throughout his dictatorship, Benito Mussolini had promoted

888
00:51:08,521 --> 00:51:11,260
as a way to mobilize Italy.

889
00:51:11,360 --> 00:51:13,600
Mussolini was very fond of motorcycles,

890
00:51:13,700 --> 00:51:16,100
and obviously for good reason.

891
00:51:16,200 --> 00:51:18,860
Italy was a great production center,

892
00:51:18,960 --> 00:51:24,040
and he wanted to make his followers into a nation

893
00:51:24,140 --> 00:51:28,000
of motorized centaurs.

894
00:51:28,100 --> 00:51:30,440
By war's end in Italy, both Mussolini

895
00:51:30,540 --> 00:51:34,040
and his motorcycles were out of favor.

896
00:51:34,140 --> 00:51:38,520
But war-battered Italy was in need of cheap transportation.

897
00:51:38,521 --> 00:51:42,660
So Corradino Descania designed a vehicle that wasn't quite a motorcycle.

898
00:51:42,860 --> 00:51:43,560
Like this.

899
00:51:44,160 --> 00:51:47,900
I began by drawing a person, sitting comfortably on a seat.

900
00:51:47,901 --> 00:51:52,880
I drew two wheels and handlebars above the front wheel.

901
00:51:53,800 --> 00:51:55,540
Automatically, it took shape.

902
00:51:56,980 --> 00:51:59,600
Descania's scooter came out in 1946.

903
00:52:00,380 --> 00:52:03,540
It was called the Wasp, or in Italian, Vespa.

904
00:52:03,541 --> 00:52:09,040
With automatic transmissions and unintimidating power,

905
00:52:09,140 --> 00:52:12,280
Vespas were effortless to ride.

906
00:52:12,380 --> 00:52:14,280
And with all that swoopy sheet metal,

907
00:52:14,380 --> 00:52:16,780
they embodied sensual Italian design.

908
00:52:21,200 --> 00:52:25,200
A Vespa was a machine that tripped the light fantastic,

909
00:52:25,300 --> 00:52:28,500
just like Gregory Peck and Audrey Hepburn in Roman Holiday.

910
00:52:28,501 --> 00:52:36,440
holiday. But was it a motorcycle?

911
00:52:36,540 --> 00:52:39,340
The scooter's tiny wheels and step-through riding positions

912
00:52:39,440 --> 00:52:43,040
separated it from the real bikes.

913
00:52:43,140 --> 00:52:45,620
And scooters don't have the long-standing, hardcore

914
00:52:45,720 --> 00:52:48,460
following that motorcycles do.

915
00:52:48,560 --> 00:52:51,960
Scooters are fashion, not passion.

916
00:52:52,060 --> 00:52:54,260
Here's the ever-inspiring Angie Dickinson.

917
00:52:54,360 --> 00:52:56,800
Useless trying to tell me that it's the wasps that sit on

918
00:52:56,801 --> 00:52:58,040
that sit on flowers.

919
00:52:58,140 --> 00:53:00,740
No, it's the flowers that sit on the wasps.

920
00:53:00,840 --> 00:53:01,940
It's a strange thing.

921
00:53:02,040 --> 00:53:05,040
They're practical, but they're also fashionable.

922
00:53:05,140 --> 00:53:08,540
And so they go in and out of fashion.

923
00:53:08,640 --> 00:53:11,540
In England during the 60s, scooters and motorcycles

924
00:53:11,640 --> 00:53:14,860
didn't mix at all.

925
00:53:14,960 --> 00:53:19,520
It was the mods and the rockers, the scooter set versus the cycle

926
00:53:19,620 --> 00:53:21,600
cult.

927
00:53:21,700 --> 00:53:24,800
Well, the rockers were the more, I guess, in American terms,

928
00:53:24,801 --> 00:53:27,800
the Fonzie type guys with the leather jackets

929
00:53:27,900 --> 00:53:30,400
and the faster bikes.

930
00:53:30,500 --> 00:53:34,440
The mods would be guys in scooters

931
00:53:34,540 --> 00:53:38,040
that were more fashion conscious.

932
00:53:38,140 --> 00:53:40,180
The mod look is seen in a production still

933
00:53:40,280 --> 00:53:44,620
from the movie Quadrophenia, starring Sting.

934
00:53:44,720 --> 00:53:46,080
Mods wore suits.

935
00:53:46,180 --> 00:53:48,360
Their scooters were heavily decorated with ribbons

936
00:53:48,460 --> 00:53:51,220
and pop graffiti.

937
00:53:51,320 --> 00:53:53,360
And they occasionally came to blows

938
00:53:53,361 --> 00:53:58,360
leather-clad rockers. Rocker bikes were known as cafe

939
00:53:58,361 --> 00:54:03,800
racers, homemade street-going versions of road racers.

940
00:54:03,801 --> 00:54:07,300
Often a cafe racer was a combination of two brands.

941
00:54:07,301 --> 00:54:10,380
A so-called Triton was a Triumph engine in a Norton

942
00:54:10,381 --> 00:54:15,820
feather bed frame. The rarer Norvin had a Norton

943
00:54:15,821 --> 00:54:20,920
frame cradling the expensive Vincent engine.

944
00:54:20,921 --> 00:54:23,480
By mixing and matching parts, British bikers

945
00:54:23,580 --> 00:54:25,320
had created a new kind of motorcycle.

946
00:54:32,160 --> 00:54:36,460
A cafe racer is really the world's first sport bike.

947
00:54:36,560 --> 00:54:40,240
Today's high-performance Repla racer sport bikes really

948
00:54:40,340 --> 00:54:42,380
started with the cafe racers.

949
00:54:45,320 --> 00:54:47,920
The name cafe racer comes from the British ritual

950
00:54:47,921 --> 00:54:50,920
racing from one transport cafe to the next.

951
00:54:54,760 --> 00:54:57,660
A typical run would be from the Ace Cafe of London

952
00:54:57,760 --> 00:55:04,360
to the Chelsea Bridge and back, through traffic at speeds

953
00:55:04,460 --> 00:55:07,080
exceeding 100 miles an hour.

954
00:55:07,180 --> 00:55:08,980
That was the big performance benchmark.

955
00:55:09,080 --> 00:55:10,980
You would start out at the Ace Cafe.

956
00:55:11,080 --> 00:55:15,780
You drop your money in the jukebox, and you get on your bike,

957
00:55:15,781 --> 00:55:19,580
and you try to get back before one of the longer songs of the

958
00:55:19,581 --> 00:55:22,380
era had played. And to do that, of course, you

959
00:55:22,381 --> 00:55:26,960
had to do the ton. Ton up fast and loaded with

960
00:55:26,961 --> 00:55:30,220
charisma, Brit bikes dominated motorcycling.

961
00:55:33,260 --> 00:55:36,500
Yet by the early 60s in America, there was already a contender in

962
00:55:36,501 --> 00:55:41,100
sight. Of all things, it was an undersized

963
00:55:41,101 --> 00:55:44,540
bike from a country that at the time was synonymous with cheap

964
00:55:44,541 --> 00:55:46,540
to keep goods.

965
00:55:46,640 --> 00:55:48,740
To think that you could come here with a small machine

966
00:55:48,840 --> 00:55:53,680
of 50 cc's and succeed seemed absurd to most people.

967
00:55:53,780 --> 00:55:57,980
The man behind the machine was no stranger to failure.

968
00:55:58,080 --> 00:56:02,000
So a Chiro Honda was a college dropout whose first career

969
00:56:02,100 --> 00:56:05,000
as race car driver ended in this crash.

970
00:56:08,200 --> 00:56:10,900
He eventually found success making small engines

971
00:56:11,000 --> 00:56:13,400
for mounting on bicycles.

972
00:56:13,401 --> 00:56:17,100
helped to mobilize post-war Japan.

973
00:56:17,200 --> 00:56:21,440
By 1959, Honda was exporting his small is better philosophy

974
00:56:21,540 --> 00:56:25,840
to America in the form of the Honda Cub.

975
00:56:25,940 --> 00:56:29,440
The feisty 50cc bike had a step-through riding position,

976
00:56:29,540 --> 00:56:31,760
like a scooter.

977
00:56:31,860 --> 00:56:34,260
But with full-sized motorcycle wheels,

978
00:56:34,360 --> 00:56:38,360
it had automatic transmission and 3 and 1 half horsepower,

979
00:56:38,460 --> 00:56:42,200
all for $195.

980
00:56:42,201 --> 00:56:47,620
And people started to realize what performance these little machines had, and they were built

981
00:56:47,621 --> 00:56:49,960
like a fine watch.

982
00:56:49,961 --> 00:56:52,120
And they were incredibly durable.

983
00:56:52,121 --> 00:56:53,900
They were fun to ride.

984
00:56:53,901 --> 00:56:57,720
They sipped fuel at an amazingly low rate.

985
00:56:57,721 --> 00:57:00,440
And so they slowly began to catch on.

986
00:57:00,441 --> 00:57:06,640
What helped them catch on was a nearly irresistible ad campaign.

987
00:57:06,641 --> 00:57:09,820
Bikers at that time were known as mad, bad, and dangerous to know.

988
00:57:09,821 --> 00:57:12,660
Your mom didn't want you to associate or be a biker.

989
00:57:13,000 --> 00:57:16,160
And so for Honda to be able to break that perception,

990
00:57:16,560 --> 00:57:19,320
they had to create this great advertising campaign

991
00:57:19,321 --> 00:57:21,740
which said you meet the nicest people on a Honda.

992
00:57:21,960 --> 00:57:23,260
And the Honda you met them on

993
00:57:23,261 --> 00:57:25,240
was this little red and cream Honda 50.

994
00:57:26,460 --> 00:57:27,460
When I was a kid,

995
00:57:28,180 --> 00:57:29,920
the hottest selling motorcycle in the world

996
00:57:29,921 --> 00:57:30,880
was the Honda 50.

997
00:57:31,960 --> 00:57:33,400
I remember Honda came to this country

998
00:57:33,401 --> 00:57:34,120
and they told the dealers,

999
00:57:34,260 --> 00:57:35,780
we want to sell 5,000 of these.

1000
00:57:35,860 --> 00:57:36,560
And the dealers said,

1001
00:57:36,561 --> 00:57:37,700
you'll never sell 5,000 a year.

1002
00:57:37,740 --> 00:57:39,620
They said, no, no, we want to sell 5,000 a month.

1003
00:57:39,820 --> 00:57:40,860
Oh, it never happened.

1004
00:57:41,080 --> 00:57:41,780
But they did.

1005
00:57:41,781 --> 00:57:42,760
They sold millions of them.

1006
00:57:43,560 --> 00:57:45,660
The Cub was followed by other bikes,

1007
00:57:46,280 --> 00:57:50,660
the 160cc Honda Dream, the 305 Superhawk.

1008
00:57:52,980 --> 00:57:55,100
The only thing missing from the Honda lineup

1009
00:57:55,101 --> 00:57:57,100
was a big displacement road burner.

1010
00:57:57,920 --> 00:58:00,760
In 1969, that changed.

1011
00:58:01,540 --> 00:58:04,440
I was walking through the exhibition in Las Vegas

1012
00:58:04,441 --> 00:58:07,940
not so long ago, and we passed the CB750 Honda,

1013
00:58:08,340 --> 00:58:09,040
the Honda 4.

1014
00:58:09,041 --> 00:58:12,840
And I heard a guy just near me saying, ah, the bike that

1015
00:58:12,940 --> 00:58:14,440
changed the world.

1016
00:58:14,540 --> 00:58:16,840
And in many ways, the CB750 was the bike that

1017
00:58:16,940 --> 00:58:18,680
changed the world.

1018
00:58:18,780 --> 00:58:20,580
The inline four-cylinder engine

1019
00:58:20,680 --> 00:58:21,780
was new for the street.

1020
00:58:25,800 --> 00:58:29,500
Disc brakes were a motorcycling first.

1021
00:58:29,600 --> 00:58:33,500
And the brawny styling was brand new for Honda.

1022
00:58:33,600 --> 00:58:36,040
I got on it the first time, and I looked down at the fuel

1023
00:58:36,140 --> 00:58:39,040
tank, and everything just seemed massive.

1024
00:58:39,041 --> 00:58:43,040
And it just felt like it had just run a hole right through the wind.

1025
00:58:45,180 --> 00:58:46,740
What was the British response?

1026
00:58:48,040 --> 00:58:50,120
If you met the nicest people on a Honda,

1027
00:58:50,580 --> 00:58:52,140
you met a certain girl on a Norton.

1028
00:58:53,720 --> 00:58:56,180
The Norton girl was, you know,

1029
00:58:56,220 --> 00:58:59,700
only probably the most well-remembered advertising campaign

1030
00:58:59,701 --> 00:59:01,580
in all of motorcycle history.

1031
00:59:01,581 --> 00:59:03,920
This was the late 60s, early 70s,

1032
00:59:03,921 --> 00:59:07,320
and Norton hit upon the idea

1033
00:59:07,321 --> 00:59:12,960
of putting a very beautiful, very British-looking bird,

1034
00:59:13,380 --> 00:59:15,840
as they would say back then, with their motorcycles

1035
00:59:15,841 --> 00:59:16,840
in a full-page ad.

1036
00:59:18,980 --> 00:59:21,640
But if you looked past the girl to the motorcycle,

1037
00:59:22,360 --> 00:59:24,300
you saw an increasingly dated machine,

1038
00:59:25,100 --> 00:59:28,080
a 60s street racer built with 40s technology.

1039
00:59:29,980 --> 00:59:33,980
And old-time Triumph and BSA buffs will tell you

1040
00:59:33,981 --> 00:59:36,260
that the bikes of the 40s and 50s

1041
00:59:36,261 --> 00:59:40,080
were very sweet running and docile and lasted well,

1042
00:59:40,860 --> 00:59:43,300
and that as they got into the 60s,

1043
00:59:43,320 --> 00:59:44,480
they started becoming something

1044
00:59:44,481 --> 00:59:46,260
they really weren't engineered to be,

1045
00:59:46,420 --> 00:59:49,540
which was fire-breathing, high-performance motorcycles.

1046
00:59:51,060 --> 00:59:54,500
The 1970s were hard times in general in England.

1047
00:59:55,460 --> 00:59:58,120
The Labour Party in its so-called nanny state

1048
00:59:58,121 --> 01:00:00,800
put much of the workforce on the dole

1049
01:00:00,801 --> 01:00:03,740
and spread apathy throughout business,

1050
01:00:04,500 --> 01:00:05,840
including that of motorcycles.

1051
01:00:06,260 --> 01:00:08,260
The English blew it.

1052
01:00:08,360 --> 01:00:10,860
You know, they did not improve their product enough,

1053
01:00:10,960 --> 01:00:13,560
and they didn't pay attention enough.

1054
01:00:18,500 --> 01:00:22,540
One by one during the 70s, the old English brands failed.

1055
01:00:22,640 --> 01:00:27,880
The last holdout, Triumph, closed its doors in 1984.

1056
01:00:27,980 --> 01:00:30,960
And even some who were steeped in the romance of British iron

1057
01:00:31,060 --> 01:00:34,380
had to admit there was a new world order.

1058
01:00:34,381 --> 01:00:39,380
I felt sort of that I had been set free, that I had...

1059
01:00:39,480 --> 01:00:42,380
I loved the Norton, but I couldn't trust it.

1060
01:00:42,480 --> 01:00:46,220
And when I sold it and got the Honda, all of a sudden I felt

1061
01:00:46,320 --> 01:00:49,260
that I could get out a map of the United States and look at

1062
01:00:49,360 --> 01:00:51,560
it and go anywhere.

1063
01:00:51,660 --> 01:00:54,460
At the moment the Japanese were redefining the street

1064
01:00:54,560 --> 01:00:57,640
bike in the 70s, a revolution was also taking place in the

1065
01:00:57,740 --> 01:00:58,640
dirt.

1066
01:00:58,740 --> 01:01:04,320
Motocross morphed into Supercross and was the best

1067
01:01:04,380 --> 01:01:06,780
launched skyward into the freestyle era.

1068
01:01:13,180 --> 01:01:15,660
It's often said that riding a motorcycle fast

1069
01:01:15,760 --> 01:01:18,820
feels like flying.

1070
01:01:18,920 --> 01:01:22,440
But one kind of riding isn't like flying.

1071
01:01:22,540 --> 01:01:23,360
It is flying.

1072
01:01:25,940 --> 01:01:28,380
I like riding street bikes too.

1073
01:01:28,480 --> 01:01:30,200
But you can't jump a street bike.

1074
01:01:30,300 --> 01:01:32,440
I want to be up in the air.

1075
01:01:32,441 --> 01:01:36,940
But was a motorcycle really meant to do this?

1076
01:01:37,040 --> 01:01:40,480
And how do we get from bouncing over a dirt track

1077
01:01:40,580 --> 01:01:42,080
to backflips in midair?

1078
01:01:46,080 --> 01:01:49,520
It began in the 1940s with a cross-country race

1079
01:01:49,620 --> 01:01:53,300
Americans called a scramble.

1080
01:01:53,400 --> 01:01:55,900
What's a motorcycle scramble?

1081
01:01:56,000 --> 01:01:58,200
Man, it's crazy.

1082
01:01:58,300 --> 01:02:01,800
Take a bunch of cool cats on bouncing bikes.

1083
01:02:01,801 --> 01:02:03,700
All for the fun.

1084
01:02:03,800 --> 01:02:07,800
And if this is fun, son, I don't care if you take my share.

1085
01:02:11,040 --> 01:02:14,040
In the 1960s, America adopted European rules

1086
01:02:14,140 --> 01:02:18,880
for the race, along with a name, motocross country,

1087
01:02:18,980 --> 01:02:20,380
or just motocross.

1088
01:02:25,360 --> 01:02:28,360
Moto is their word for motorcycle.

1089
01:02:28,361 --> 01:02:32,360
And to basically save space in newspaper ads,

1090
01:02:32,460 --> 01:02:37,060
motocross country became just moto X.

1091
01:02:37,160 --> 01:02:41,500
Motocross rules dictated that a course should be irregular,

1092
01:02:41,600 --> 01:02:46,220
so that both right and left turns are negotiated.

1093
01:02:46,320 --> 01:02:49,520
Hills and jumps were included not for show,

1094
01:02:49,620 --> 01:02:52,020
but so that riders would be forced to change gears.

1095
01:02:55,160 --> 01:02:56,920
Motocross races were normally held

1096
01:02:56,921 --> 01:03:00,520
deep in the countryside, making it hard to attract large

1097
01:03:00,620 --> 01:03:01,620
audiences to races.

1098
01:03:04,620 --> 01:03:06,860
But if people wouldn't come to motocross,

1099
01:03:06,960 --> 01:03:09,660
motocross would come to them.

1100
01:03:09,760 --> 01:03:12,900
In the early 70s, promoters started building artificial

1101
01:03:13,000 --> 01:03:14,480
courses in football stadiums.

1102
01:03:17,580 --> 01:03:21,020
Motocross was a natural fit for a stadium, where finally,

1103
01:03:21,120 --> 01:03:24,520
spectators could take in all the action.

1104
01:03:24,521 --> 01:03:27,160
But strictly speaking, a race on an artificial course

1105
01:03:27,260 --> 01:03:30,460
was no longer motocross.

1106
01:03:30,560 --> 01:03:33,460
What to call it?

1107
01:03:33,560 --> 01:03:37,260
An exhibition staged in the Los Angeles Coliseum in 1972

1108
01:03:37,360 --> 01:03:40,940
was called the Super Bowl of Motocross.

1109
01:03:41,040 --> 01:03:43,580
The contraction, Supercross, stuck.

1110
01:03:48,280 --> 01:03:51,780
In the confines of the stadium, the style of racing changed.

1111
01:03:51,880 --> 01:03:54,520
There wasn't space for much of a straightaway.

1112
01:03:54,521 --> 01:03:58,520
But there was plenty of room to go vertical.

1113
01:03:58,521 --> 01:04:00,520
Supercross is more about jumping.

1114
01:04:00,521 --> 01:04:03,520
Motocross is more about going fast.

1115
01:04:03,521 --> 01:04:07,520
Supercross is more of a spectacle.

1116
01:04:07,521 --> 01:04:09,520
For the fan, you can sit around the track,

1117
01:04:09,521 --> 01:04:11,520
you can see all of the racing.

1118
01:04:11,521 --> 01:04:13,520
There's big jumps.

1119
01:04:13,521 --> 01:04:15,520
People like to show off.

1120
01:04:15,521 --> 01:04:18,520
And sometimes the racing's a lot better

1121
01:04:18,521 --> 01:04:20,520
because it's more tight.

1122
01:04:20,521 --> 01:04:22,520
Supercross is not just a race.

1123
01:04:22,521 --> 01:04:26,900
is not just a race, it's an event.

1124
01:04:27,000 --> 01:04:28,700
While the races are different, the machines

1125
01:04:28,800 --> 01:04:32,400
are basically the same.

1126
01:04:32,500 --> 01:04:38,740
There are still two basic engine classifications, 125 cc

1127
01:04:38,840 --> 01:04:44,380
and 250 cc. Tiny compared with street bike engines,

1128
01:04:44,480 --> 01:04:49,380
these mills are brutally effective for the job at hand.

1129
01:04:49,381 --> 01:04:52,820
Dirt bikes traditionally have two-stroke engines.

1130
01:04:52,920 --> 01:04:55,620
In a two-stroke, the up and down movement of the piston

1131
01:04:55,720 --> 01:04:58,720
opens the exhaust port and intake port,

1132
01:04:58,820 --> 01:05:00,920
eliminating the need for the intake and exhaust

1133
01:05:01,020 --> 01:05:04,020
strokes of the four-stroke engine.

1134
01:05:04,120 --> 01:05:07,040
There's a power pulse at every revolution of the crankshaft,

1135
01:05:07,140 --> 01:05:08,600
unlike the four-stroke engine, which

1136
01:05:08,700 --> 01:05:10,240
produces power every two turns.

1137
01:05:13,600 --> 01:05:16,780
And since it lacks the valves and camshaft of a four-stroke,

1138
01:05:16,781 --> 01:05:20,280
The whole package is lighter.

1139
01:05:20,380 --> 01:05:25,680
A 250 two-stroke was many times faster, quicker,

1140
01:05:25,780 --> 01:05:27,220
and less tiring on the rider.

1141
01:05:27,320 --> 01:05:29,720
Very important when you're racing 40 minutes

1142
01:05:29,820 --> 01:05:30,720
than a big four-stroke.

1143
01:05:34,740 --> 01:05:37,540
And yet a two-stroke has a fatal flaw.

1144
01:05:37,640 --> 01:05:41,380
It burns a mixture of oil and gas called premix.

1145
01:05:41,480 --> 01:05:45,340
Burning oil produces large quantities of air pollution.

1146
01:05:45,341 --> 01:05:47,740
The two-stroke engine is inherently dirtier than a

1147
01:05:47,840 --> 01:05:50,240
four-stroke.

1148
01:05:50,340 --> 01:05:52,740
Two-stroke engines have been unable to meet clean air

1149
01:05:52,840 --> 01:05:55,880
regulations since the mid-'80s and are no longer used on

1150
01:05:55,980 --> 01:05:58,580
street bikes.

1151
01:05:58,680 --> 01:06:01,380
They may one day vanish from dirt bikes.

1152
01:06:01,480 --> 01:06:05,200
500-cc four-stroke bikes called thumpers have become

1153
01:06:05,300 --> 01:06:09,200
competitive with 250 two-strokes.

1154
01:06:09,300 --> 01:06:12,440
The Japanese market for four-strokes is catching up

1155
01:06:12,441 --> 01:06:15,680
lightness and the balance of a two-stroke right now.

1156
01:06:15,780 --> 01:06:18,840
Two, three years from now, I think a four-stroke will be

1157
01:06:18,940 --> 01:06:22,880
just as good as a two-stroke, if not better.

1158
01:06:22,980 --> 01:06:26,380
If motocross has the European racing pedigree,

1159
01:06:26,480 --> 01:06:30,360
Supercross gave it a touch of American showbiz.

1160
01:06:30,460 --> 01:06:33,160
But the true exhibitionists of the dirt were still waiting

1161
01:06:33,260 --> 01:06:34,360
in the wings.

1162
01:06:37,860 --> 01:06:40,460
The wild aerobatics of freestyle motocross

1163
01:06:40,461 --> 01:06:44,400
actually came from bikes without a motor.

1164
01:06:44,500 --> 01:06:47,100
BMX, or bicycle motocross competitions,

1165
01:06:47,200 --> 01:06:49,100
began in the 1970s.

1166
01:06:49,200 --> 01:06:53,200
Kids not old enough to drive were catching air on their bikes.

1167
01:06:53,300 --> 01:06:58,340
Among them, future Supercross champion Jeremy McGrath.

1168
01:06:58,440 --> 01:07:00,740
And midway through his record string of Supercross

1169
01:07:00,840 --> 01:07:03,120
victories, McGrath decided to try something he'd learned

1170
01:07:03,220 --> 01:07:08,620
in his BMX days, a stunt called a knack-knack.

1171
01:07:08,621 --> 01:07:10,620
I was a little nervous to test it out in the US,

1172
01:07:10,621 --> 01:07:14,620
so I had a European Supercross in Spain in 1994.

1173
01:07:14,621 --> 01:07:17,620
I did my first knack-knack.

1174
01:07:17,621 --> 01:07:21,620
It got quite big reviews for sure.

1175
01:07:21,621 --> 01:07:23,620
The people didn't know what to think of it.

1176
01:07:23,621 --> 01:07:25,620
I know what my parents thought of it.

1177
01:07:25,621 --> 01:07:28,620
They were freaked out for sure.

1178
01:07:28,621 --> 01:07:32,620
It became a ritual for McGrath and other riders

1179
01:07:32,621 --> 01:07:35,620
to celebrate every win with a BMX-style stunt.

1180
01:07:35,621 --> 01:07:40,620
All the showboating led to expression sessions held during

1181
01:07:40,621 --> 01:07:43,620
the halftime of Supercross meets.

1182
01:07:43,621 --> 01:07:47,620
By 1998, freestyle motocross was a standalone sport,

1183
01:07:47,621 --> 01:07:51,620
filling up stadiums around the world,

1184
01:07:51,621 --> 01:07:54,620
even a famous bullfight arena.

1185
01:07:54,621 --> 01:07:58,620
Plaza de Toro in Madrid, Spain, 25,000 people showed up

1186
01:07:58,621 --> 01:08:00,620
to watch freestyle motocross to entertain them.

1187
01:08:00,621 --> 01:08:04,620
And they told me that the biggest crowd they ever had

1188
01:08:04,621 --> 01:08:08,160
The biggest crowd they've ever had there was about 12,000

1189
01:08:08,260 --> 01:08:11,120
for a bullfight.

1190
01:08:11,220 --> 01:08:14,120
The freestyle repertoire includes the bar hop,

1191
01:08:14,220 --> 01:08:16,560
where the rider shoots his legs over the handlebars.

1192
01:08:19,060 --> 01:08:22,540
Seat grab Indian Air is two tricks in one,

1193
01:08:22,640 --> 01:08:25,540
a seat grab with a scissors-like motion of the legs

1194
01:08:25,640 --> 01:08:26,600
called Indian Air.

1195
01:08:29,940 --> 01:08:33,020
There's the Superman.

1196
01:08:33,021 --> 01:08:36,420
and a flying handstand called the heart attack,

1197
01:08:36,520 --> 01:08:41,160
the signature stunt of top freestyler Cary Hart.

1198
01:08:41,260 --> 01:08:43,660
What freestylers would call the gnarliest trick

1199
01:08:43,760 --> 01:08:44,560
is the backflip.

1200
01:08:48,560 --> 01:08:50,680
You're seeing the first time it was ever attempted

1201
01:08:50,780 --> 01:08:54,380
in competition by Cary Hart in 2000.

1202
01:08:54,480 --> 01:08:56,780
What you can't quite see is Hart's crash landing.

1203
01:08:59,780 --> 01:09:03,020
Later, Mike Metzger learned how to stick the landing.

1204
01:09:03,021 --> 01:09:05,320
So what's it like to launch yourself over backwards

1205
01:09:05,420 --> 01:09:08,320
on a motorcycle?

1206
01:09:08,420 --> 01:09:11,320
For that split second, you can't see nothing,

1207
01:09:11,420 --> 01:09:14,460
except you're just waiting for the ground to come around.

1208
01:09:14,560 --> 01:09:16,960
And it's like, OK, there's the ground.

1209
01:09:17,060 --> 01:09:19,260
Time to land.

1210
01:09:19,360 --> 01:09:20,780
That was fun.

1211
01:09:20,880 --> 01:09:23,680
I want to do it again.

1212
01:09:23,780 --> 01:09:25,880
And how dangerous is this?

1213
01:09:25,980 --> 01:09:28,980
Mike the godfather Metzger has broken both legs,

1214
01:09:29,080 --> 01:09:32,320
lost eight teeth, suffered three broken backs,

1215
01:09:32,321 --> 01:09:39,300
was badly scarred by a spinning tire, all before the age of 27.

1216
01:09:39,400 --> 01:09:42,060
But that was when he was racing Supercross.

1217
01:09:42,160 --> 01:09:46,260
Metzger believes freestyle is safe by comparison.

1218
01:09:46,360 --> 01:09:50,940
Right now, you know, I'd really prefer to go out and do as many

1219
01:09:51,040 --> 01:09:54,480
backflips as I can compared to going and racing with 20 guys

1220
01:09:54,580 --> 01:09:57,480
on a starting gate.

1221
01:09:57,580 --> 01:09:59,680
From the heavy traffic of Supercross

1222
01:09:59,681 --> 01:10:01,680
to one-on-one freestyling.

1223
01:10:01,780 --> 01:10:03,920
It's Little Dirt Bikes, Big Air.

1224
01:10:14,420 --> 01:10:15,820
It's the naked machine.

1225
01:10:18,040 --> 01:10:22,340
All its workings on display, designed and evolved

1226
01:10:22,440 --> 01:10:25,580
to be seen as well as performed.

1227
01:10:25,680 --> 01:10:28,940
Most motorcycles, you look at it, you see the engine,

1228
01:10:28,941 --> 01:10:31,840
the wheels, you see the brakes.

1229
01:10:31,841 --> 01:10:36,340
In essence, the mechanicals are on display, whereas most cars

1230
01:10:36,341 --> 01:10:39,340
these days, even when you open the hood, you're hard pressed

1231
01:10:39,341 --> 01:10:41,880
to see anything mechanical.

1232
01:10:41,881 --> 01:10:44,980
That motorcycles are mechanical art of a high level

1233
01:10:44,981 --> 01:10:48,200
is something bikers take for granted.

1234
01:10:48,201 --> 01:10:51,400
But it took until the early 90s for the art establishment

1235
01:10:51,401 --> 01:10:53,400
to catch on.

1236
01:10:53,401 --> 01:10:55,500
About 10 years ago, Thomas Kranz,

1237
01:10:55,501 --> 01:10:57,940
the director of the Guggenheim Museum in New York,

1238
01:10:57,941 --> 01:11:01,940
of New York, talked to me about making an exhibition of

1239
01:11:01,941 --> 01:11:03,440
motorcycles.

1240
01:11:03,441 --> 01:11:04,940
I laughed at the time.

1241
01:11:04,941 --> 01:11:07,940
And then a couple of years later he called me and he said,

1242
01:11:07,941 --> 01:11:10,940
I'm going to do this motorcycle exhibition as our end of

1243
01:11:10,941 --> 01:11:13,940
century exhibition.

1244
01:11:13,941 --> 01:11:17,940
A few snickers were also heard from the cultural elite.

1245
01:11:17,941 --> 01:11:25,940
Among certain art circles, there was a poo-pooing of, you

1246
01:11:25,941 --> 01:11:28,740
How can a motorcycle be artistic?

1247
01:11:28,840 --> 01:11:32,740
How can a motorcycle be considered as worthy of being

1248
01:11:32,840 --> 01:11:35,040
installed in the Guggenheim Museum?

1249
01:11:39,280 --> 01:11:43,300
The exhibit opened in June 1998.

1250
01:11:43,400 --> 01:11:46,500
Critics were won over by the display of the motorcycle

1251
01:11:46,600 --> 01:11:50,300
as a 20th century conduit for design, art, and culture.

1252
01:11:50,301 --> 01:11:57,300
culture. And museum patrons voted with their feet.

1253
01:11:57,301 --> 01:12:01,540
The old phrase, a blockbuster, literally means that people line

1254
01:12:01,541 --> 01:12:03,540
up around the block to try and get in.

1255
01:12:03,541 --> 01:12:07,660
And this was a blockbuster for the Guggenheim.

1256
01:12:07,661 --> 01:12:10,860
The end of the line would join the front of the line virtually

1257
01:12:10,861 --> 01:12:12,260
right around the New York City block.

1258
01:12:15,900 --> 01:12:19,300
110 motorcycles were on display.

1259
01:12:19,301 --> 01:12:24,300
from the 1868 steam velocipede to modern sport bikes.

1260
01:12:24,301 --> 01:12:26,600
How did a bike make the final cut?

1261
01:12:26,601 --> 01:12:30,260
The official criteria said it had to have three things,

1262
01:12:30,261 --> 01:12:38,460
aesthetic merit, technical innovation, and social impact.

1263
01:12:38,461 --> 01:12:41,700
Yet at least one bike, a replica of Peter Fonda's chopper

1264
01:12:41,701 --> 01:12:46,340
from Easy Rider, made it on sheer notoriety.

1265
01:12:46,341 --> 01:12:48,700
The Captain America bike is a great example

1266
01:12:48,701 --> 01:12:53,660
of how our criteria for selecting bikes applied and didn't apply at the same time.

1267
01:12:53,661 --> 01:12:57,020
The Captain America bike is virtually devoid of any technical merit.

1268
01:12:57,980 --> 01:13:01,980
It's a horrible thing to ride, but it is a wonderful thing to look at.

1269
01:13:03,820 --> 01:13:08,620
What we saw was that people looked at these objects as they would a sculpture.

1270
01:13:09,420 --> 01:13:17,100
We had children pressing their noses up against the bikes. We had moms and pops

1271
01:13:17,101 --> 01:13:20,940
pouring over details that would be lost on me,

1272
01:13:21,480 --> 01:13:24,120
getting pleasure out of seeing the particular shape of an engine

1273
01:13:24,121 --> 01:13:26,320
or a casting or a wheel or a tank, you know.

1274
01:13:26,480 --> 01:13:27,980
And that was very exciting to see.

1275
01:13:30,500 --> 01:13:31,980
For the bikers in the crowd,

1276
01:13:32,240 --> 01:13:34,620
this was vindication for all the years

1277
01:13:34,621 --> 01:13:37,140
they spent in their garages admiring their cycles.

1278
01:13:40,000 --> 01:13:42,980
The Guggenheim, which is as famous a building

1279
01:13:42,981 --> 01:13:44,820
as there is in the world,

1280
01:13:44,821 --> 01:13:48,620
exhibited in this almost hallowed ground,

1281
01:13:48,720 --> 01:13:51,420
this Frank Lloyd Wright structure that everybody

1282
01:13:51,520 --> 01:13:52,960
in the world knows.

1283
01:13:53,060 --> 01:13:55,560
It really was a moment.

1284
01:13:58,360 --> 01:14:03,880
So here we have the motorcycle being recognized as art,

1285
01:14:03,980 --> 01:14:05,580
which is wonderful.

1286
01:14:05,680 --> 01:14:09,280
Plus, it drives all the snobs nuts, which is as much fun.

1287
01:14:12,280 --> 01:14:14,820
When the exhibit traveled to Bilbao, Spain,

1288
01:14:14,821 --> 01:14:18,260
It opened with a celebrity biker run.

1289
01:14:18,360 --> 01:14:22,820
Lawrence Fishburne, Jeremy Irons, Dennis Hopper,

1290
01:14:22,920 --> 01:14:27,760
and Lauren Hutton were among the motorcycle glitterati.

1291
01:14:27,860 --> 01:14:30,500
The exhibition drew 2 million people at its runs

1292
01:14:30,600 --> 01:14:33,800
in Europe and America.

1293
01:14:33,900 --> 01:14:36,440
Still, it was just one indication of the new way

1294
01:14:36,540 --> 01:14:40,480
that people had come to look at motorcycles.

1295
01:14:40,580 --> 01:14:42,940
The exhibition, The Art of the Motorcycle,

1296
01:14:42,941 --> 01:14:44,940
was successful on its own terms.

1297
01:14:45,040 --> 01:14:46,740
But what was really fascinating to me

1298
01:14:46,840 --> 01:14:49,940
was that it did capture a particular moment in the popular

1299
01:14:50,040 --> 01:14:52,280
thinking about motorcycles.

1300
01:14:52,380 --> 01:14:54,680
Motorcycles now in America are considered

1301
01:14:54,780 --> 01:14:56,780
to be a desirable object.

1302
01:14:56,880 --> 01:14:59,080
And that's the first time that that's

1303
01:14:59,180 --> 01:15:01,500
been the case, universally.

1304
01:15:01,600 --> 01:15:04,300
Could it be that the outlaw machine was becoming

1305
01:15:04,400 --> 01:15:05,100
respectable?

1306
01:15:07,600 --> 01:15:09,100
This seemed far-fetched.

1307
01:15:09,200 --> 01:15:10,700
The brand that spearheaded the change

1308
01:15:10,800 --> 01:15:12,300
seemed even more unlikely.

1309
01:15:12,301 --> 01:15:17,300
The 1970s have been hard times for Harley-Davidson

1310
01:15:17,400 --> 01:15:21,040
and its line of heavyweight motorcycles.

1311
01:15:21,140 --> 01:15:23,380
There wasn't a lot of competition in that class,

1312
01:15:23,480 --> 01:15:26,020
so they just made the same bike over and over again.

1313
01:15:26,120 --> 01:15:28,580
And they, new hand grip for 74.

1314
01:15:28,680 --> 01:15:30,280
You know, I mean, those are the kind of improvements

1315
01:15:30,380 --> 01:15:31,780
that you had.

1316
01:15:31,880 --> 01:15:33,460
They didn't get any better.

1317
01:15:33,560 --> 01:15:35,720
And consequently, they lost market share

1318
01:15:35,820 --> 01:15:39,520
because they kept making the same thing over and over again.

1319
01:15:39,620 --> 01:15:42,300
Harley was a family business no more than ever.

1320
01:15:42,301 --> 01:15:47,440
In 1969, the motor company had been sold to AMF, the bowling

1321
01:15:47,540 --> 01:15:49,040
equipment company.

1322
01:15:49,140 --> 01:15:52,040
AMF tried to match the flood of Japanese imports

1323
01:15:52,140 --> 01:15:54,540
by pumping up production.

1324
01:15:54,640 --> 01:15:57,540
But more bikes turned out to mean less quality.

1325
01:15:59,760 --> 01:16:02,860
If you bought an AMF Harley, you might find yourself pushing

1326
01:16:02,960 --> 01:16:03,960
it rather than riding.

1327
01:16:07,660 --> 01:16:11,500
In 1981, a group of Harley executives, including

1328
01:16:11,501 --> 01:16:15,800
including a grandson of one of the founders, Willie G. Davidson,

1329
01:16:15,900 --> 01:16:20,640
bought the struggling company from AMF.

1330
01:16:20,740 --> 01:16:23,180
But the technological seed of Harley's turnaround

1331
01:16:23,280 --> 01:16:25,980
was already germinating.

1332
01:16:26,080 --> 01:16:29,060
Under AMF's guidance and leadership,

1333
01:16:29,160 --> 01:16:31,520
the new Evolution motor was started.

1334
01:16:31,620 --> 01:16:33,660
They green-lighted the project.

1335
01:16:33,760 --> 01:16:38,660
They knew enough to know that the product had to improve.

1336
01:16:38,760 --> 01:16:41,500
The Evolution engine, shown here in the sports

1337
01:16:41,501 --> 01:16:47,954
version looked like the classic Harley V-Twin, but the engineering was all new.

1338
01:16:47,955 --> 01:16:49,180
The top end was

1339
01:16:49,181 --> 01:16:53,639
fabricated with aluminum rather than iron. Tolerances were closer.

1340
01:16:53,640 --> 01:16:55,260
Cooling action improved.

1341
01:16:58,540 --> 01:17:03,820
A friend of mine who was a Harley dealer said that essentially they went from a 29,000 mile

1342
01:17:03,821 --> 01:17:09,660
engine to a 100,000 mile engine overnight in terms of its service life without needing any real work.

1343
01:17:11,500 --> 01:17:17,560
So here the Harley is, and it looks like your grandfather's motorcycle, but it's got electric start.

1344
01:17:18,140 --> 01:17:20,516
It's got good brakes. It's going to start every time.

1345
01:17:20,517 --> 01:17:22,220
It's a wonderful piece of engineering.

1346
01:17:24,340 --> 01:17:28,600
And suddenly, every guy of a certain age seemed to want a Harley.

1347
01:17:30,720 --> 01:17:35,900
If you looked closely enough, it was the same generation that fueled the bike boom of the 1960s.

1348
01:17:38,060 --> 01:17:41,100
Schooled on Hondas, they graduated to Harleys.

1349
01:17:41,500 --> 01:17:44,200
Maybe they rode bikes when they were teenagers,

1350
01:17:44,300 --> 01:17:47,640
but they put that aside to concentrate on the career track,

1351
01:17:47,740 --> 01:17:48,800
the family.

1352
01:17:48,900 --> 01:17:52,040
And when that was taken care of,

1353
01:17:52,140 --> 01:17:54,140
they thought they deserved a little fun.

1354
01:17:57,040 --> 01:18:00,760
That word fun underscores almost everything in motorcycling.

1355
01:18:00,860 --> 01:18:03,660
Any big boom in motorcycling, the word fun

1356
01:18:03,760 --> 01:18:06,300
is somewhere in that equation.

1357
01:18:06,400 --> 01:18:08,920
And this time around, there was no mom and dad

1358
01:18:08,921 --> 01:18:11,420
to read the riot act about motorcycles.

1359
01:18:17,920 --> 01:18:20,160
For many people now, it is a wonderful irony

1360
01:18:20,260 --> 01:18:23,260
that they are able to buy the bike that their parents

1361
01:18:23,360 --> 01:18:24,660
wouldn't let them have.

1362
01:18:24,760 --> 01:18:27,480
And that's very liberating.

1363
01:18:27,580 --> 01:18:30,480
There's one bike that even your mom might approve of.

1364
01:18:30,580 --> 01:18:33,480
So it may surprise you to learn of its hooligan past.

1365
01:18:33,481 --> 01:18:37,980
past.

1366
01:18:38,080 --> 01:18:40,120
It has long been true that the next big thing

1367
01:18:40,220 --> 01:18:42,620
in motorcycling may come from the street rather

1368
01:18:42,720 --> 01:18:43,420
than the factory.

1369
01:18:47,420 --> 01:18:53,540
The cafe racer came out of a London biker hangout,

1370
01:18:53,640 --> 01:18:57,980
a chopper from some nameless garage.

1371
01:18:58,080 --> 01:19:00,580
It even happened to Honda's flagship touring machine,

1372
01:19:00,680 --> 01:19:03,480
the Goldwing.

1373
01:19:03,481 --> 01:19:05,320
The Goldwing is a very interesting motorcycle

1374
01:19:05,420 --> 01:19:07,380
because it started out as one thing

1375
01:19:07,480 --> 01:19:08,420
and has turned into another.

1376
01:19:11,280 --> 01:19:13,120
In its original iteration, the Goldwing

1377
01:19:13,220 --> 01:19:16,220
was seen by the folks at Honda as being a big muscle bike,

1378
01:19:16,320 --> 01:19:18,820
a big performance bike.

1379
01:19:18,920 --> 01:19:23,040
The 1975 Goldwing was a bare bones bike.

1380
01:19:23,140 --> 01:19:28,940
No plastic bodywork, just a massive mill and a stout frame.

1381
01:19:29,040 --> 01:19:32,140
But its smooth running, liquid cooled four cylinder engine

1382
01:19:32,141 --> 01:19:34,640
turned out to be perfect for the long haul.

1383
01:19:35,200 --> 01:19:39,120
Almost from the get-go, Goldwing owners loaded them up with touring essentials.

1384
01:19:41,260 --> 01:19:43,340
They put their own fairings up front.

1385
01:19:43,440 --> 01:19:46,060
You could buy aftermarket fairings, handlebar mount fairings.

1386
01:19:47,400 --> 01:19:49,200
They put their own saddlebags on.

1387
01:19:49,600 --> 01:19:51,260
They put their own tour trunks on.

1388
01:19:51,340 --> 01:19:52,600
They fitted it with radios.

1389
01:19:52,601 --> 01:19:54,980
They fitted it with crude cruise controls.

1390
01:19:56,000 --> 01:19:58,960
A few short years after the Goldwing's introduction,

1391
01:19:58,961 --> 01:20:03,960
Honda realized that their customers had redesigned their motorcycle.

1392
01:20:03,961 --> 01:20:08,960
And basically, we simply followed the customer's direction.

1393
01:20:08,961 --> 01:20:10,960
And the machines began to evolve.

1394
01:20:10,961 --> 01:20:16,960
We began to offer fairings and bags and some of these touring accessories and equipment as well.

1395
01:20:16,961 --> 01:20:21,960
And then by the early 1980s, we began to make the whole package.

1396
01:20:21,961 --> 01:20:27,960
The 1980 Interstate was the first fully loaded Goldwing.

1397
01:20:27,961 --> 01:20:31,200
The Goldwing was gradually upsized and loaded with

1398
01:20:31,300 --> 01:20:36,560
accessories all the way until 2001, when Honda offered a

1399
01:20:36,660 --> 01:20:39,000
sporty, streamlined version.

1400
01:20:39,100 --> 01:20:42,400
And so the wing's muscle bike roots finally caught up with

1401
01:20:42,500 --> 01:20:46,320
its reputation for luxury touring.

1402
01:20:46,420 --> 01:20:49,620
The new Goldwing, they should have called it the Sportwing

1403
01:20:49,720 --> 01:20:53,160
because it really is an amazing motorcycle.

1404
01:20:53,260 --> 01:20:56,120
The thing will fly around corners like a sport bike,

1405
01:20:56,121 --> 01:21:00,120
bike, stop really good, accelerate really hard,

1406
01:21:00,220 --> 01:21:03,860
and has a stereo and cup holders and cruise control

1407
01:21:03,960 --> 01:21:04,860
at the same time.

1408
01:21:04,960 --> 01:21:07,860
And the only kind of bike I can get my wife on back of is like

1409
01:21:07,960 --> 01:21:11,860
a Goldwing, because she can doze off back there.

1410
01:21:11,960 --> 01:21:15,080
If the Goldwing was reinvented in the garage,

1411
01:21:15,180 --> 01:21:17,480
the single most stunning bike of the 1990s

1412
01:21:17,580 --> 01:21:21,880
had an even more surprising origin.

1413
01:21:21,980 --> 01:21:25,380
One man, John Britton, working in New Zealand, which has no

1414
01:21:25,381 --> 01:21:28,020
There's no history of motorcycling, incidentally,

1415
01:21:28,021 --> 01:21:32,020
created with his own hands a motorcycle that was not only

1416
01:21:32,021 --> 01:21:36,700
a thing of incredible beauty with its lipstick pink and powder

1417
01:21:36,701 --> 01:21:40,420
blue color scheme and those beautiful organic aerodynamic

1418
01:21:40,421 --> 01:21:47,980
shapes and an amazing engine, but it also went like the wind.

1419
01:21:47,981 --> 01:21:52,100
This is the Britton V1000, a bike whose performance

1420
01:21:52,101 --> 01:21:57,840
lines are clouded only by personal tragedy.

1421
01:21:57,940 --> 01:22:00,900
John Britton was both an engineer and artist who had

1422
01:22:01,000 --> 01:22:06,340
designed a successful line of original glass art.

1423
01:22:06,440 --> 01:22:09,560
But his true passion was motorcycle racing.

1424
01:22:09,660 --> 01:22:13,260
In 1990, Britton began building his own race bike from the

1425
01:22:13,360 --> 01:22:17,600
ground up, engine and all, wrapping it in the liquid lines

1426
01:22:17,700 --> 01:22:20,260
of a glass artist.

1427
01:22:20,261 --> 01:22:24,900
In 1994, Britton took his V1000 to Daytona.

1428
01:22:25,000 --> 01:22:27,840
And I immediately tracked him down and said,

1429
01:22:27,940 --> 01:22:30,740
we must have this motorcycle on the cover

1430
01:22:30,840 --> 01:22:33,200
of Cycle World magazine.

1431
01:22:33,300 --> 01:22:37,240
To my mind, that Britton V1000 was the most innovative

1432
01:22:37,340 --> 01:22:40,980
motorcycle that has come down the pike in many, many years,

1433
01:22:41,080 --> 01:22:41,880
maybe ever.

1434
01:22:44,880 --> 01:22:46,760
The most innovative feature of Britton's bike

1435
01:22:46,761 --> 01:22:50,520
was the frame, or lack thereof.

1436
01:22:50,521 --> 01:22:54,000
Instead of the usual steel or aluminum backbone,

1437
01:22:54,001 --> 01:22:58,400
Britton used the engine block as the frame.

1438
01:22:58,401 --> 01:23:00,760
Tank, wheels, and all were connected to the engine

1439
01:23:00,761 --> 01:23:03,760
by organically shaped carbon fiber and Kevlar pieces.

1440
01:23:07,760 --> 01:23:10,760
The V1000 was beginning to win on the international race

1441
01:23:10,761 --> 01:23:15,760
circuit when, in 1995, Britton died of malignant melanoma.

1442
01:23:15,761 --> 01:23:17,760
melanoma.

1443
01:23:17,761 --> 01:23:20,760
He was 45.

1444
01:23:20,761 --> 01:23:24,760
He was a man who was lost to motorcycling forever after.

1445
01:23:24,761 --> 01:23:31,760
But that one moment, the Britton V1000, seems to me to capture all of the qualities that

1446
01:23:31,761 --> 01:23:33,760
we love about motorcycling.

1447
01:23:33,761 --> 01:23:39,760
The personal endeavor, the beauty, the pure beauty, and the exhilarating performance.

1448
01:23:39,761 --> 01:23:44,760
Fortunately, John Britton had no lack of company when it came to rethinking the motorcycle.

1449
01:23:44,761 --> 01:23:49,760
We really started with the concept of, I want to make the

1450
01:23:49,761 --> 01:23:52,260
sport bike chassis take a leap forward.

1451
01:23:52,261 --> 01:23:54,760
I'm not satisfied where everything is today.

1452
01:23:54,761 --> 01:23:58,760
Former Harley-Davidson engineer Eric Buell left the motor

1453
01:23:58,761 --> 01:24:01,760
company in 1983 to build race bikes.

1454
01:24:01,761 --> 01:24:05,760
In 1987, he began building sport bikes using Harley-Davidson

1455
01:24:05,761 --> 01:24:07,760
Sportster engines.

1456
01:24:07,761 --> 01:24:11,760
It is much like, you know, the Chevy V8 or the Fender

1457
01:24:11,761 --> 01:24:13,760
Stratocaster in the music industry, the one that everybody

1458
01:24:13,761 --> 01:24:16,860
that everybody keeps making copies of.

1459
01:24:16,960 --> 01:24:19,460
In 2002, Buell started from scratch

1460
01:24:19,560 --> 01:24:22,860
with a new V-twin engine and a revolutionary frame.

1461
01:24:25,300 --> 01:24:29,300
One of the duties of the frame is to carry the gas tank.

1462
01:24:29,400 --> 01:24:32,220
The gas tank sits atop the frame, usually.

1463
01:24:32,320 --> 01:24:36,820
Well, Eric said, why don't we make the frame the gas tank?

1464
01:24:36,920 --> 01:24:38,320
Brilliant.

1465
01:24:38,420 --> 01:24:41,760
On this motorcycle, this is the air box cover.

1466
01:24:41,761 --> 01:24:45,400
The fuel is all inside this massive aluminum frame here.

1467
01:24:45,500 --> 01:24:47,660
It fills through here and goes down both sides of the frame

1468
01:24:47,760 --> 01:24:49,700
and across the back.

1469
01:24:49,800 --> 01:24:53,700
Fuel also eliminated the typical oil tank.

1470
01:24:53,800 --> 01:24:55,300
We came up with the idea of putting the oil

1471
01:24:55,400 --> 01:24:57,200
right in the very front of the swing arm.

1472
01:24:57,300 --> 01:24:59,320
The swing arm is an aluminum casting,

1473
01:24:59,420 --> 01:25:00,920
which dissipates heat really well.

1474
01:25:01,020 --> 01:25:02,720
There's a lot of cool air flowing by it.

1475
01:25:02,820 --> 01:25:05,320
It gives us a lot of volume and a great location,

1476
01:25:05,420 --> 01:25:09,420
and it takes the weight away of a separate tank.

1477
01:25:09,421 --> 01:25:11,820
With no separate oil and fuel tanks,

1478
01:25:11,920 --> 01:25:14,720
the Firebolt is shorter and lighter.

1479
01:25:14,820 --> 01:25:16,520
And with all the heavy liquids inside,

1480
01:25:16,620 --> 01:25:19,760
not on top of the frame, there's a lower center of gravity,

1481
01:25:19,860 --> 01:25:23,260
riveting the bike to the road.

1482
01:25:23,360 --> 01:25:25,260
You can find the Firebolt and other Buells

1483
01:25:25,360 --> 01:25:27,680
at Harley-Davidson dealerships.

1484
01:25:27,780 --> 01:25:30,700
Since 1998, Harley has controlled the majority share

1485
01:25:30,800 --> 01:25:31,880
of the Buell Motor Company.

1486
01:25:35,020 --> 01:25:36,780
Another machine has gone even further

1487
01:25:36,781 --> 01:25:41,080
in rejiggering the traditional geometry of two wheels.

1488
01:25:41,180 --> 01:25:45,320
This is the Segway.

1489
01:25:45,420 --> 01:25:48,460
Instead of the usual bicycle-style inline wheels,

1490
01:25:48,560 --> 01:25:50,720
they're side by side, as on a hand cart.

1491
01:25:55,140 --> 01:25:58,740
Not only that, all the usual controls are absent.

1492
01:25:58,840 --> 01:26:02,680
Riding the Segway is completely intuitive.

1493
01:26:02,780 --> 01:26:06,240
If you want to go forward, you lean forward.

1494
01:26:06,241 --> 01:26:10,480
You want to go backward, you lean backward.

1495
01:26:10,580 --> 01:26:12,680
There's no brakes, there's no throttle.

1496
01:26:12,780 --> 01:26:15,820
To turn to the left, you rotate the handle to the left.

1497
01:26:15,920 --> 01:26:19,120
To turn to the right, you rotate the handle to the right.

1498
01:26:19,220 --> 01:26:20,260
That's all there is to it.

1499
01:26:23,720 --> 01:26:26,620
But as top speed is only 12 miles an hour,

1500
01:26:26,720 --> 01:26:31,900
nobody's calling the Segway a motorcycle, at least not yet.

1501
01:26:32,000 --> 01:26:35,300
What starts out as a utilitarian device, like the Segway,

1502
01:26:35,301 --> 01:26:38,040
You know, we're Americans, and we'll hot rod anything.

1503
01:26:38,140 --> 01:26:40,700
I could certainly see the day where, you know,

1504
01:26:40,800 --> 01:26:44,740
maybe there's Segway racing or Segway trials.

1505
01:26:44,840 --> 01:26:46,740
And so some things haven't changed

1506
01:26:46,840 --> 01:26:51,140
since the beginning of motorized two wheeling.

1507
01:26:51,240 --> 01:26:54,560
They've been bobbed and hot rodded,

1508
01:26:54,660 --> 01:27:02,260
bred in the race pits, battle tested in the dirt,

1509
01:27:02,261 --> 01:27:07,460
and pieced together from basket cases in 1,000 garages.

1510
01:27:07,560 --> 01:27:10,760
I'm only thinking about the one I'm working on right now.

1511
01:27:10,860 --> 01:27:12,600
This one.

1512
01:27:12,700 --> 01:27:15,300
That's the one I care about right now.

1513
01:27:15,400 --> 01:27:21,320
And at the moment it seems locked in place,

1514
01:27:21,420 --> 01:27:24,620
someone pulls off another stunning variation

1515
01:27:24,720 --> 01:27:27,860
on the theme of two wheels and a motor.

1516
01:27:27,960 --> 01:27:30,920
The fact that it only is two wheels and an engine

1517
01:27:30,921 --> 01:27:34,420
You've got to make the best of a very simple shape.

1518
01:27:34,520 --> 01:27:37,360
And that brings out the best in designers.

1519
01:27:37,460 --> 01:27:40,100
More than just a machine, it's a symbol

1520
01:27:40,200 --> 01:27:43,800
of a deep divide in the way we go places.

1521
01:27:43,900 --> 01:27:47,940
There have always been riders and drivers,

1522
01:27:48,040 --> 01:27:51,940
those who need the comfort and safety of the coach,

1523
01:27:52,040 --> 01:27:55,640
and those who crave the freedom of the saddle.

1524
01:27:55,740 --> 01:27:58,540
You see it one day, and you say, that's for me.

1525
01:27:58,541 --> 01:28:01,580
And there's one of the mysteries of the human race

1526
01:28:01,680 --> 01:28:03,780
that we don't know why that happens,

1527
01:28:03,880 --> 01:28:05,880
but it certainly hits some of us real hard.

1528
01:28:10,880 --> 01:28:14,560
I don't necessarily want to know what it is.

1529
01:28:14,660 --> 01:28:16,660
I just know that it works, and I enjoy it,

1530
01:28:16,760 --> 01:28:18,660
and I like having a bit of the mystery.

1531
01:28:22,660 --> 01:28:24,900
And now, throttle back.

1532
01:28:25,000 --> 01:28:27,740
Let that charismatic, enigmatic machine

1533
01:28:27,741 --> 01:28:30,780
machine cool off.

1534
01:28:30,880 --> 01:28:33,180
Maybe even take a breather yourself

1535
01:28:33,280 --> 01:28:35,280
and dream about your next ride.

1536
01:29:27,740 --> 01:29:29,800
you

